// Positioned markers for the icons whose prose reading lives in hotspots2.js.
//
// One entry per Commons file title: [label, explanation, top%, left%]. The coordinates are
// measured off the picture itself with `python3 src/tools/grid.py <outdir> <key>`, which
// overlays a 10% grid on `src/img/<key>.webp` — never guessed from a thumbnail, or the
// marker lands on empty sky. Keep every coordinate inside 9–92: assemble.js clamps outside
// that, and `make check` reports the clamp.
//
// Every explanation is either a detail actually visible in the frame read as the Fathers
// read it, or the standard grammar of Orthodox iconography (the cruciform nimbus with Ο ΩΝ,
// the ΙΣ ΧΣ and ΜΡ ΘΥ monograms, veiled hands, hierarchic scale, the axe at the root).
// Nothing here is invented symbolism.
module.exports={

'File:Icon with the Virgin of the Tree of Jesse. 1666 (8384481474).jpg':[
 ["The Theotokos in the vine","The tree grows to her and stops. The Church sings Isaiah's word of her at every feast of the Mother of God: there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots — she is the rod, and the Child she holds is the Branch.","26%","49%"],
 ["The Child in her lap","Matthew's forty-two generations do not end in a name but in a birth: Jacob begat Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ. Everything painted below this point exists for what is painted here.","36%","62%"],
 ["David crowned, with the psaltery","He is given his crown, his harp and a scroll of his own words. Matthew's list turns twice at him — fourteen generations from Abraham to David, and fourteen from David to the carrying away into Babylon.","24%","24%"],
 ["A prophet's open scroll","The scroll beside this prophet carries in Greek the very verse Matthew quotes at the Nativity: Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and shall bear a son. In the text the genealogy is a list; in the icon it is a company of witnesses, each holding the words by which he foretold the Child.","41%","73%"],
 ["Jesse asleep at the root","The old man lies at the foot of the icon with the trunk rising at his side. He is the father of David, and he sleeps: the generations that carried the promise did not see what would grow out of them.","80%","23%"]],

'File:Tree of Jesse icon (17 c.).jpg':[
 ["The Theotokos at the summit","The central column ends here, in the Mother of God standing on a pedestal with the Child, in dark blue against the ochre. She is standing, not enthroned. The panel is read from the bottom upward, as the genealogy is read from Abraham forward.","10%","48%"],
 ["The foot of the panel","There is no reclining Jesse here and no trunk: the stem simply comes down to the base band, where a blackened inscription plate hangs and a small figure bows beneath it. This painter has kept the vine as scaffolding for the roundels and dropped the root that gives the icon its usual name.","88%","48%"],
 ["A generation in its own roundel","Every forefather sits inside a thin roundel of vine with a small red rosette where the tendrils meet, turned toward the next. Matthew's begat, repeated forty-two times, is painted here as one plant with many branches rather than as a list.","46%","20%"],
 ["The prophets in the lower band","Along the base stand the prophets with their scrolls, outside the vine and pointing into it. The genealogy is Israel's history; the scrolls are Israel's expectation of how it would end.","87%","33%"]],

'File:031 Nativity of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["The ray from heaven","A single shaft comes down the cleft of the rock to the place where the Child lies. It has no source inside the picture: the light of this night is not one of the lights of the created world.","30%","50%"],
 ["The angels above the cave","Ranks of angels lean out of the cloud over the whole width of the icon. The feast's own hymn assigns each creature its gift — the angels bring their singing, the heavens a star, the earth a cave, and mankind a Virgin Mother.","10%","25%"],
 ["The shepherds and the angel sent to them","At the left an angel bends down to men keeping watch on the hillside. They are the poor and the unlettered, and they are told first.","25%","20%"],
 ["The Magi on the road","At the right the Magi are still riding. The icon of the feast holds the whole mystery in one field, so the men whose coming Matthew reports in the next chapter are already on their way.","26%","77%"],
 ["The Theotokos","This painter does not lay her down. She kneels at the left of the manger with her hands crossed on her breast and her head bowed to it, doing nothing and asking nothing — the Church sings that she gives birth to him who is before the ages, and that both the birth and the virginity are true.","54%","36%"],
 ["The Child in the stone manger","He is bound in swaddling bands and laid in a manger painted as a stone chest. The icon of the Nativity already carries the shape of the Burial: he is wrapped and laid in stone at the beginning as he will be at the end.","73%","55%"],
 ["Joseph","Matthew tells the Nativity through Joseph — the just man who was minded to put her away privily, and who did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him. He is painted haloed, bowed, and silent: in the Gospel he never speaks a word.","54%","66%"]],

'File:12 Nativity of Jesus Icon in Assumption of Mary Church in Agios Vasileios.jpg':[
 ["The shaft of light","A broad orange shaft comes down the middle of the board from the top edge to the manger. Nothing the panel still shows is at the top of it: whatever the painter set there — a star, a segment of heaven — has been rubbed away.","14%","49%"],
 ["The messenger and the shepherd","At the upper right a figure in white raises his hand over a man leaning on a staff on the hill. No wings survive on him, but that is the announcement to the shepherds, and it is Luke who reports it, not Matthew.","20%","72%"],
 ["What the rubbing took","There is a scene in this corner to balance the one opposite, and it is gone: a knot of small figures in pink and blue with nothing left to identify them by. An Orthodox Nativity usually puts the Magi here, and that is a guess about this panel, not a reading of it.","23%","18%"],
 ["The Theotokos","She kneels at the right of the manger in the rose maphorion that marks her in every icon, under a halo ringed with gold dots, her hands crossed on her breast rather than holding the Child. The Church calls her the first of those who worship him.","54%","63%"],
 ["Joseph","The old man in blue bows at the head of the manger. Matthew's account is his: the dream, the angel's word, and the obedience that took Mary as his wife and called the Child Jesus.","56%","31%"],
 ["The Child on the white masonry","A block of white stone with a straight top and a moulded lip, and the Child lying along the top of it in rose swaddling bands. The panel is too far gone to show whether it has a far rim. Orthodox iconography gives the crib the form of a tomb, so that the Nativity is never seen apart from why he was born.","76%","47%"]],

'File:Nativity Icon Panagia Evraidos Church 19 Century.jpg':[
 ["The angels at the head of the icon","Four angels fill the upper register, bending over the scene from the other side of the world. Glory to God in the highest is their part in the feast, and the icon gives them the highest place in the panel.","10%","25%"],
 ["The shepherds on the hillside","A man with his pipe raises his hand at the news, and the inscribed band above the hill names the shepherds. The Church remembers that the message was given first to men at work in the fields at night.","33%","78%"],
 ["Joseph","He sits at the left in red, haloed and turned toward the Child. Matthew's Nativity is told from where Joseph stands — the just man to whom the angel came in a dream and said, fear not to take unto thee Mary thy wife.","50%","30%"],
 ["The Theotokos","She kneels at the right in the rose maphorion, her hand stretched toward the Child. In this icon she does not hold him: the Church paints her giving him, not keeping him.","48%","65%"],
 ["The Child on the ground, wrapped in white","He lies swaddled with the light breaking out around him, laid on the bare earth. The Word by whom all things were made is painted needing to be wrapped up and put down somewhere.","70%","45%"],
 ["The head behind Joseph","Looked at close, this is a young man in profile — brow, nose and chin, with dark curling hair — leaning in past Joseph's red shoulder to see the Child, not the ox or the ass. There is no beast anywhere in this panel, which is unusual: the ox and the ass come into the icon from Isaiah, not from any Gospel, and this painter left them out.","64%","10%"],
 ["The two beasts at the foot of the icon","Two pale animals lie on the grass at the very bottom, nose to tail, the nearer with a long muzzle and a drooping ear. They belong to the hillside above, where the shepherds are; the feast is set among ordinary things and the icon does not tidy them out of the way.","88%","54%"]],

'File:Cathedral (Monreale) - North arch of transept - Adoration by the Magi.jpg':[
 ["The star and its ray","The star hangs in a disc at the top of the field and sends one long ray down to the house. St John Chrysostom argued from Matthew that this was no ordinary star: it moved from east to south, it was seen by day, and it came down low enough to stand over one roof.","8%","33%"],
 ["The inscription","Four lines of Latin stand in the gold at the upper left, abbreviated as the mosaicists wrote them — MAGI MUNERA OFFERUT DNO IESU XPO, the Magi offer gifts to the Lord Jesus Christ. Monreale was worked by Byzantine craftsmen for a Norman king, so the images are Greek and the titles Latin.","17%","20%"],
 ["The three gifts","Three gold vessels shaped like jewelled crowns are held out together across the middle of the mosaic, green and red stones set into their rims. St Gregory the Great reads them as a confession: gold, as to a King; frankincense, as sacrifice to God; myrrh, as embalming the body of one who is to die. St Augustine says the same in almost the same words.","50%","38%"],
 ["The Magi themselves","Three men of three ages — a beardless youth in front, a brown-bearded man behind him, a white-bearded elder at the back with his head wrapped in white, the only one of them covered. St Augustine calls them the first fruits of the Gentiles: Israelite shepherds and Gentile Magi, one from near and one from afar, hastening to the same Corner-stone.","46%","17%"],
 ["The Theotokos enthroned","She is seated as on a throne and holds the Child on her lap toward them. Matthew says they saw the young child with Mary his mother, and fell down, and worshipped him — the icon keeps mother and Son together, because it was by her that he could be found.","33%","78%"],
 ["The Child reaching out","He sits on his Mother's knee, turns toward the gifts and puts out his hand open, the fingers spread — reaching, not making the sign of blessing. He is painted as a real child, and what the Magi kneel to is not the promise of a king but a King already come.","46%","70%"]],

'File:Cathedral (Monreale) - North arch of transept - Journey of the Magi.jpg':[
 ["The star in the disc of heaven","The star is set in a dark blue medallion and a beam runs down from it across the gold. Matthew's words are the whole of what the Magi knew: we have seen his star in the east, and are come to worship him.","8%","57%"],
 ["The inscription","The mosaic letters it in abbreviation with strokes over the shortened words — ...TES · STELLĀ · IN · ORIĒTE · VENT — for MAGI VIDENTES STELLAM IN ORIENTE VENIUNT IN HIERUSALEM, the Magi, seeing the star in the east, come to Jerusalem. St Leo notes what is wrong with that: judging as men, they looked for a new-born King in the royal city, and the star had to leave them until the scribes named Bethlehem.","17%","18%"],
 ["The youngest of the three","The Magi are painted as three ages of man, which is why one rider is beardless. The Church did not know their names from the Gospel; what she keeps is that they were Gentiles, and that they came a long way.","32%","30%"],
 ["The eldest","The white-bearded rider between the other two. St Jerome supposed they came by the prophecy of Balaam, that there should come a Star out of Jacob — an oracle spoken among the Gentiles and remembered by them.","31%","52%"],
 ["The hand raised toward the star","Each of them turns and points upward, and the gesture, not the road, is the subject. St Gregory the Great says the Jews, who used their reason, were called by an angel's preaching, while the Gentiles, who did not, were called by a sign.","27%","74%"]],

'File:Menologion of Basil II - Flight into Egypt.jpg':[
 ["The Theotokos on the ass","She rides side-saddle on a borrowed animal, out of the land of the promise into the land of the plagues. St John Chrysostom points at the speed of it: as soon as he is born the tyrant is furious against him, and the mother with her Child is driven into a foreign country.","43%","34%"],
 ["The Child held in her arms","He is carried; he does nothing. St Bede says he practised first what he would later command — when they persecute you in this city, flee ye into another — and fled before the face of man, he whom a star had just proclaimed from heaven.","55%","36%"],
 ["Joseph going ahead on foot","The angel's word was to Joseph and it was exact: take the young child and his mother, and flee into Egypt. He walks ahead with the ass's rein in his hand, grey-bearded and barefoot, setting the pace — an old man walking the road he was told to walk. He carries no bundle: that is on the young man's staff behind.","48%","58%"],
 ["The young man with the staff","A youth strides along behind, at the tail of the beast, a staff over his shoulder with the bundle knotted to it. Church tradition identifies him as James, the son of Joseph, whom the Gospel calls the Brother of the Lord; he is painted in this scene from the earliest Byzantine examples onward.","50%","14%"],
 ["The walled city of Egypt","The journey ends at a walled town, and out of its open door steps a figure in a small gold crown carrying a dark bundle and something gilt — the city itself come out to meet them, which is how Byzantine painters put a town into a picture. St Augustine turns the whole flight around at this point: Moses shut the light of day out from the Egyptians, and Christ by going down thither brought light back to them that sat in darkness — he fled in order to enlighten them, not to escape his enemies.","32%","82%"]],

'File:Flight into Egypt Icon Mary Evraida Church Kastoria.jpg':[
 ["The Greek band across the top","The dark band above the hills carries the name of the scene in Greek. Orthodox icons are titled: an image is not an icon of an event until the event is written on it.","12%","50%"],
 ["The Theotokos","She rides in the red maphorion, her face turned out of the picture rather than at the road. St Hilary notes that after the birth Matthew stops calling her the espoused wife and calls her only the mother of Jesus.","40%","39%"],
 ["The Child held against her","The angel's word puts him first — take the young child and his mother — and the icon does the same: everything in the frame is arranged around a Child too small to hold on by himself.","49%","46%"],
 ["The ass","The Lord of all travels on a borrowed beast, as he will again on the way into Jerusalem. Orthodox painting keeps the poverty of the story rather than smoothing it into a royal progress.","65%","33%"],
 ["Joseph with the staff","He walks ahead, haloed, turning back toward them. Matthew's chapter 2 is a series of dreams given to Joseph and obeyed at once: he arose, and took the young child and his mother by night, and departed.","44%","76%"]],

'File:Cathedral (Monreale) - South arch of transept - Flight into Egypt.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","The Latin names the whole scene — Christ, with Mary his mother and Joseph, enters into Egypt. The Gospel gives the reason in one line: that it might be fulfilled which was spoken of the Lord by the prophet, Out of Egypt have I called my son.","10%","40%"],
 ["The Child on Joseph's shoulders","He rides on the old man's shoulders and blesses with his right hand as he goes. The mosaic holds both together: a refugee child being carried, and the Lord blessing the country he is entering.","14%","57%"],
 ["Joseph carrying him","Matthew tells this whole chapter from Joseph's obedience. St Bede reads the flight as the lot of the elect, who are often driven from their homes by the wickedness of the wicked — and Christ takes that lot first.","27%","59%"],
 ["The Theotokos riding","She rides with her hand at her breast and the Child not in her arms. She is given the beast and the road, and no explanation.","21%","30%"],
 ["The young man driving from behind","A youth in green walks at the back of the procession with a bundle slung on a pole over his shoulder and a long stick in his hand, keeping the beast moving — in tradition James, the son of Joseph, the Brother of the Lord. The flight is painted as a household on the move.","23%","12%"],
 ["Egypt","The buildings at the end of the road are the country of the ten plagues. St Hilary observes what it means that Christ, after being sought among the Jews, leaves Judaea to be sheltered among nations full of idols.","40%","82%"]],

'File:Flight into Egypt (12 Sinai).jpg':[
 ["The red Greek caption","A caption in red runs above the scene, naming the flight into Egypt. This panel is a detail cut from a whole Nativity icon at St Catherine's on Sinai, where the flight is one moment of the feast among several.","14%","45%"],
 ["The Theotokos and the Child on the ass","She holds him with both hands as the beast walks. St John Chrysostom draws the lesson for the reader from exactly this: if at the beginning of your own course you meet with trouble, do not be discouraged, but bear it, having this before you.","33%","37%"],
 ["Joseph leading them on","He walks in front in red, haloed, looking back. Matthew says he departed by night; St Jerome notices that the Gospel mentions night for the going down and says nothing of darkness for the return.","35%","58%"],
 ["The young man with the staff","The youth behind the beast is the son of Joseph whom tradition names James, the Brother of the Lord. He belongs to this scene in Byzantine painting as regularly as the ass does.","35%","22%"],
 ["Rachel weeping","A woman in dark robes stands in the doorway at the right with her hands raised, and the red letters written under her name her: ΡΑΧΗΛ ΚΛΑΙΟΥΣΑ. She is not part of the journey — she is the next paragraph, set beside it on the same board. In Rama was there a voice heard, lamentation, and weeping, and great mourning, Rachel weeping for her children, and would not be comforted, because they are not.","48%","77%"]],

'File:Massacre of the Innocents (Ionian, 17th c., Byzantine museum).jpg':[
 ["Herod in the portico","Two bearded men stand back under the colonnade at the edge of the scene, the front one in a red patterned robe, with a written placard hung on the column beside them. No crown is painted on either of them, and no enthroned king appears anywhere else in the panel: this is where the icon puts the man who gave the order and did not carry it out. St John Chrysostom explains the fury: the Magi had obeyed an infant lying in a manger, whom the king on his throne could not move — and when a king's anger is stirred by fear for his crown, it is inextinguishable.","22%","11%"],
 ["The soldiers with drawn swords","Matthew's sentence is short and the icon is not: he sent forth, and slew all the children that were in Bethlehem, and in all the coasts thereof, from two years old and under. St Jerome notes that the order reached beyond the town to the villages round it.","36%","26%"],
 ["The mothers","They fill the lower half of the panel, holding and pleading. St Jerome divides the prophecy between the two: the weeping is the cry of the children, the lamentation is the mothers' — in the children the cry ends with their death, in the mothers it begins again with every remembrance.","65%","20%"],
 ["The angels carrying the children up","At the upper right angels rise on a cloud with small children in their arms. St Augustine says that while Herod persecuted Christ he furnished him an army of martyrs clothed in white robes, of the Lord's own age; the Church keeps them on the 29th of December as the first martyrs.","19%","76%"],
 ["The slain at the foot of the icon","The bodies are painted along the bottom edge, where the reader cannot look past them. Orthodox painting does not soften this: Matthew set the massacre four verses after the adoration of the Magi, and the feast keeps it four days after the Nativity.","84%","45%"]],

'File:Menologion of Basil II - Massacre of the Innocents.jpg':[
 ["Herod enthroned","He sits crowned and robed at the left, in the dress of a Byzantine emperor. The disc of light behind his head is the manuscript's mark of a reigning king and not a sign of holiness — the Menologion gives it to rulers as such.","29%","20%"],
 ["The guard at his side","The order is passed from the throne to armed men. St Augustine's remark on the whole business is that this unrighteous enemy could never have profited these infants by his love as much as he did by his hate.","40%","36%"],
 ["The soldier with the drawn sword","He strides out of the palace toward the children with the blade already out. Matthew names the measure Herod used — from two years old and under, according to the time which he had diligently enquired of the wise men.","52%","52%"],
 ["The child held up by the leg","The manuscript paints the act itself rather than its aftermath. This is the horror St John Chrysostom says the Evangelist means the reader to feel before he is told that the Prophet had foretold it.","55%","62%"],
 ["The mother at the right","She sits apart, bowed over what is left to her. In Ramah was there a voice heard, lamentation, and weeping, and great mourning, Rachel weeping for her children, and would not be comforted, because they are not.","65%","87%"],
 ["The bodies on the ground","The children are laid along the lower edge of the gold field. St Bede reads the death of these children as the figure of the death of all Christ's martyrs, and their infancy as the sign that it is by humility alone that martyrdom is reached.","85%","65%"]],

'File:Detail of the Massacre of the innocents mosaic at Chora (1).jpg':[
 ["The soldier stooping over the child","The mosaicist has put the whole weight of the man over one small body. The Chora painters told the massacre at length, in several scenes, because Matthew tells it as the world's first answer to the Nativity.","45%","30%"],
 ["The child on the ground","He is drawn small, pale and unresisting against the gold. St Augustine says of these infants that only he will doubt their crown who doubts that the baptism of Christ profits infants at all.","63%","38%"],
 ["The second soldier","Another armed man closes in from the right; there is no escape drawn anywhere in the frame. The Church calls them the fourteen thousand Holy Innocents and counts them martyrs, though they died before they could speak a word of witness.","32%","82%"],
 ["The drawn sword against the gold","The blade is set high in the field where nothing hides it. St Bede's reading is that the bodies of the martyrs may be destroyed by the wicked, but that Christ cannot be taken from them.","12%","68%"]],

'File:Christos Prodomo Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["Ο ΩΝ in the arms of the halo","Three letters are set in the cross behind Christ's head: Ο ΩΝ, He Who Is — the name God gave Moses at the burning bush. No other figure in Orthodox iconography may carry it, and it is how an icon says that this man is the God of Israel.","13%","33%"],
 ["The monogram ΙΣ ΧΣ","The two pairs of letters at the top of the panel are the abbreviation of Ἰησοῦς Χριστός. An icon is not finished until it is named; the name written on it is what the veneration is offered to.","6%","55%"],
 ["The open book","The Greek on the two pages is Matthew 11:28–29, word for word: Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you, and learn of me. He does not hold a closed book of law but an open one, at an invitation.","43%","47%"],
 ["The hand raised in blessing","The fingers are formed to spell the name — the two joined and the three touching, the two natures and the Trinity. The gesture in an Orthodox icon is a confession of faith before it is a greeting.","35%","24%"],
 ["The Forerunner","He is painted gaunt and unkempt, the hair in long tangles, lettered Ο ΠΡΟΔΡΟΜΟΣ beside his halo, in a green himation over a pale streaked garment that is the camel's hair. Matthew describes him exactly so: his raiment of camel's hair, and a leathern girdle about his loins; and his meat was locusts and wild honey. St Jerome notes the point of camel's hair rather than wool — the one is the mark of austerity, the other of soft living.","13%","80%"],
 ["His hand stretched toward Christ","He stands turned inward with his right hand open toward Christ, pleading rather than preaching, and the scroll in his other hand opens with ΔΕΞΑΙ ΔΕΗΣΙΝ — receive the petition. This is the posture the Church calls the Deesis: the Forerunner and the Mother of God set on either side of the Lord, asking, which is the office he has kept since he pointed and said, Behold the Lamb of God.","30%","69%"]],

'File:St John the Baptist Late XVII - Early XVIII Cenury St Mary Blonichka Church, Ohris Icon Gallery.jpg':[
 ["The wings","The Forerunner is the one saint the Church paints with wings, and the reason is a word. Malachi's prophecy, which Christ applies to him in Matthew 11:10, calls him my messenger — in Greek ἄγγελος, angel. He is painted as what he is called.","28%","18%"],
 ["The face and the hair","Nothing is groomed. He lived in the wilderness of Judaea, and the Church remembers that the greatest born of women had no house, no office and no possessions, and that the crowds went out to him rather than he to them.","20%","48%"],
 ["The camel-hair garment","The rough coat under the cloak is the raiment Matthew names. St Hilary reads even the skins as preaching: the herald of Christ is clothed in the hide of an unclean beast, and what was unclean is made holy by being put to God's use.","45%","35%"],
 ["The scroll","The red Greek letters are his sermon, exactly as Matthew 3:2 gives it: Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand. It is the same sentence Christ himself begins to preach at Matthew 4:17 — the herald is given the King's own words.","45%","67%"],
 ["His own head on the charger","At his feet stands the dish with his severed head in it. The icon shows the end of his witness inside the picture of his preaching: he said what he was given to say, and it cost him this.","73%","68%"]],

"File:Christ's temptation (Monreale).jpg":[
 ["The tempter's words","The Latin across the top is the first temptation as Matthew records it, lettered SI FILI DI ES DIC VT LAPIDES ISTI PANE FIA — if thou be the Son of God, command that these stones be made bread. Everything the devil says in this chapter begins by putting that if into the mouth of the voice that had just said This is my beloved Son.","7%","60%"],
 ["Christ's answer","At the left the mosaic carries his reply, and it begins with the words that matter: SCRIPTUM EST — it is written — NON IN SOLO PANE VIVIT HOMO. He holds a closed scroll in his left hand while he says it. All three answers are quotations from Deuteronomy — he does not argue with the devil, he reads Scripture to him.","20%","12%"],
 ["The stones on the ground","The loose stones lie between them, in the middle of the picture. St John Chrysostom says he fasted not because he needed to but to teach us, and that this is the shield against the devil that we are meant to take up after our own baptism.","63%","55%"],
 ["The devil as a winged figure","He is painted dark, thin and winged — a fallen angel, not a beast. The temptations are addressed to the mind, so the tempter is given a mind's shape; and St Gregory the Great insists that in Christ the temptation was wholly outside him and never reached delight or consent.","33%","78%"],
 ["The wilderness","The bare rock fills the bottom of the field. St John Chrysostom notes that the devil presses hardest on a man who is alone, as he came to Eve when she was without Adam — and that Christ went out to meet him there on purpose.","78%","28%"]],

'File:Cathedral (Monreale) - Right wing transept - 2nd Temptation of Christ.jpg':[
 ["The devil's words","The Latin at the right is the second temptation: if thou be the Son of God, cast thyself down. Matthew adds what he cited to make it sound pious — the promise of Psalm 91 that God shall give his angels charge over thee.","9%","72%"],
 ["Christ's answer","At the left stands the reply — it is written again, Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God. Scripture is quoted on both sides of this mosaic; the difference is that one of them is quoting it to obey it.","10%","14%"],
 ["Christ on the pinnacle","He stands on the roof of the temple, upright and still, holding a scroll. St Gregory the Great points out that the Spirit led him there where the evil spirit would find him: the devil comes against men, but he could not come against Christ, so Christ came against the devil.","45%","33%"],
 ["The tempter beside him","He is winged and dark and reaches toward Christ without touching him. Orthodox iconography gives evil no grandeur and no beauty; it is painted small, thin and outmatched.","40%","68%"],
 ["The temple beneath his feet","The building under him is the holy place itself. The temptation was to make the house of God a stage, and the answer is that God is not to be put to the test even in his own temple.","78%","30%"]],

'File:019 Baptism of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["The light from above","A band of light comes down the middle of the panel to the head of Christ. The Father's voice and the descent of the Spirit are what give the feast its name: Theophany, the showing of God as Trinity, not merely the baptism of a man.","25%","48%"],
 ["Christ in the Jordan","He stands in the river as one condemned, with nothing on and nothing to say. St Augustine gives the reason the Church repeats at every blessing of water: he was baptized not to be cleansed himself, but to cleanse the water for us.","58%","47%"],
 ["The hand of the Forerunner","John's hand is stretched over the head of the one he had just called mightier than himself. Matthew records his protest — I have need to be baptized of thee, and comest thou to me? — and the answer that overruled it: suffer it to be so now.","47%","39%"],
 ["The angels on the bank","They wait at the right with their hands covered, holding the garments. Even the bodiless powers approach the mystery as servants approach a king, with nothing bare that could touch it.","48%","74%"],
 ["The water round him","The Jordan is painted dark and closing over him. St Ambrose recalls the psalm sung at this feast — Jordan was driven back — and says that where once the water turned back, now sin is turned back in its current.","75%","45%"]],

'File:03 Baptism of Christ Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam.jpg':[
 ["The title across the top","The Greek band names the feast. In the Orthodox calendar the 6th of January is not called the Baptism but the Theophany, because what is celebrated is that the Three were made manifest at once.","10%","45%"],
 ["The dove and the rays","The Spirit descends in the form of a dove out of an opening of light, and the rays fall on the head of Christ. The Father is heard and not painted: an icon may show only what took flesh or what was seen.","21%","50%"],
 ["Christ in the river","He stands upright in the current with the water round him. St John Chrysostom compares the two men here to the morning star and the sun: the sun does not wait for the star to set, but rises and quietly puts out its light.","46%","52%"],
 ["The Forerunner laying on his hand","He bends over the water with his hand out, the servant baptizing his Lord. St Augustine says he was baptized by John so that servants might see with what readiness they should run to the Lord's baptism, when he did not refuse to be baptized by his own servant.","36%","42%"],
 ["The angels with covered hands","Three angels stand ranked on the far bank, bowing, their hands wrapped in their garments. They hold the towels: the icon paints the whole of heaven waiting on this moment as a household waits on a king.","36%","78%"]],

'File:0663Ha. Hermitage Museum (Hall 143). Icon of the Epiphany.jpg':[
 ["The opened heaven","At the very top of the panel a dark segment of heaven is cut into the gold and a narrow ray comes down from it. Matthew says the heavens were opened unto him — the icon paints the opening itself, a hole in the sky over one man's head.","12%","47%"],
 ["The Spirit as a dove","The ray narrows to a single white bird above Christ. The Church calls this feast the Theophany because all Three are here: the Son in the water, the Spirit in the dove, and the Father in the voice that says, This is my beloved Son.","29%","47%"],
 ["Christ standing in the water","He stands with his hand extended over the river, blessing it. He does not receive holiness from the Jordan; the Jordan receives it from him, and from this baptism, the Fathers say, the blessing has flowed into every font.","55%","48%"],
 ["The Forerunner's hand on his head","John bows the whole length of his body, one hand on the head of Christ and the other still holding his own garment of hair. He had preached a baptism of repentance, and the one man who had nothing to repent of came and stood in the queue.","34%","33%"],
 ["The angels with veiled hands","Three angels lean in from the right bank with their hands hidden under their cloaks and a white cloth ready. Covered hands are the sign in Byzantine painting of what may not be touched directly.","43%","72%"],
 ["The Jordan painted as a grave","The water is a black column closing round his body from the feet up. Orthodox iconography paints the river like a tomb because that is what the Fathers make of it: he goes down into the water as into death, and everyone baptized after him goes down with him.","78%","45%"]],

'File:Elkomenos Prodromos Phylaken Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The inscription reads ΕΛΚΟΜΕΝΟΣ Ο ΠΡΟΔΡΟΜΟΣ ΕΙΣ ΤΗΝ ΦΥΛΑΚΗΝ — the Forerunner dragged into the prison. It names the exact event Matthew reports in one half-verse at 4:12, and it is why this fresco belongs here and not at chapter 11.","7%","50%"],
 ["The Forerunner","He is still in the camel-hair coat he preached in, and he is being pulled along by the wrists. The greatest of those born of women is painted powerless, and the Gospel says nothing about a rescue.","30%","48%"],
 ["The soldier's grip","One man's hands are enough to end the preaching. St John Chrysostom notes that no one can do anything against a holy man unless God gives him up, and that Christ withdrew after this rather than force the hour of his own passion.","45%","62%"],
 ["The prison","The white tower at the right is where he is going — and he is already in it: the same gaunt face under the same gold halo looks out through the grille, so the fresco shows the seizing and the shutting up at once. From that cell he will send two of his disciples with the question at Matthew 11:3 — Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another?","42%","88%"],
 ["The guard at the king's door","The armed men at the left belong to Herod, who took John because of Herodias. The icon puts the machinery of the state at one edge of the frame and one thin man at the centre.","35%","15%"]],

'File:Iomenos Asthenoun tas apo Poikilon noson.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The band across the top reads: Christ healing those that were sick of divers diseases. Matthew's summary at 4:23–24 is the whole subject — he went about all Galilee, teaching, preaching, and healing all manner of sickness among the people.","5%","50%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","He does not stand back from the crowd: the hand goes out into it, open and fingers spread, and touches nobody — a man in green reaches back and the two hands stop short of each other. A white scroll is closed in his other hand. The Fathers took this verse as the pattern for pastors — the sick were too weak to come to the physician, so the physician went round to them.","48%","38%"],
 ["The disciples behind him","They are painted close at his shoulder, watching. St John Chrysostom's comparison is to a king who gathers his army before he goes to war: the Twelve are called first, and the campaign of preaching and healing begins after.","30%","10%"],
 ["The crutches and staffs","The men at the right lean on sticks or hold them out. Matthew lists what was brought to him — those taken with divers diseases and torments, the possessed, the lunatic, the palsied — and says simply that he healed them.","60%","44%"],
 ["Those sitting on the ground","At the front of the crowd the worst off are painted lowest, sitting on the ground with their bare legs stretched out and blotched with sores, two of them in wide straw hats, their hands held out toward him. Orthodox painting keeps the poor at the front of a healing scene, where the reader has to look at them first.","78%","58%"]],

'File:IkonaZapovediBlazhenGIM.jpg':[
 ["The panel divided into compartments","The icon is ruled into rows of scenes, one for each blessing. The Beatitudes are not painted as a text on a scroll but as nine ways of living, because that is how the Fathers read them: not descriptions of feeling but of a life.","15%","12%"],
 ["The inscribed bands","The red bands between the registers carry the words of the blessings in Church Slavonic. The reader is meant to move along a band, read the sentence, and then look at the picture it belongs to.","31%","45%"],
 ["The crowds in the open country","Most of the compartments are crowded with small figures doing something to somebody — some out on the gold hills, some under painted porticoes and inside buildings, which is half the panel. St Augustine's gloss on Blessed are the meek is of that kind: the meek are they who do not resist wrongs, but overcome evil with good.","16%","62%"],
 ["A burial in the lowest register","On a gold hillside a man in a dark tunic bends over his spade and a body wound in a white shroud lies beside the opened ground, with others carrying baskets and vessels below — the mourning and the burying of the dead. St Ambrose sets the blessings in an order of learning: first to be content in poverty, and only then to govern the heart and the temper.","78%","30%"],
 ["The scale of the figures","Nobody in this icon is large. The first blessing is on the poor in spirit, and St Jerome takes them to be those who choose poverty for the Holy Spirit's sake, while St Augustine reads the phrase as the opposite of the puffed-up: the lowly, who fear God.","62%","70%"]],

'File:Christ cleans leper man.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","POTES ME MUNDARE — thou canst make me clean. The mosaic gives the leper's own words from Matthew 8:2, and they are a confession before they are a request: not wilt thou, but canst thou, and he already knows the answer.","6%","62%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","Jesus put forth his hand, and touched him — but not in this mosaic: the hand is out, the fingers formed as for speech, and between it and the leper there is a hand's breadth of bare gold. Under the Law a man who touched a leper was himself unclean until evening, and here the current runs the other way; the mosaicists have painted the instant before the touch and left the two hands apart.","44%","55%"],
 ["The leper's body","He is painted covered in spots from face to feet, and the mosaicist has not spared the reader any of them. Leprosy put a man outside the camp and outside the liturgy; this is the first person Christ touches after coming down from the mountain.","45%","80%"],
 ["The staff and the distance","He leans on a stick and stands where a leper had to stand, apart from the crowd. What Christ tells him afterwards is to go to the priest and offer what Moses commanded — the healing is not made an excuse to bypass the Law.","66%","74%"],
 ["The mountain behind them","The hill Christ has just come down from fills the left of the field. St Jerome reads the descent: on the mount only the disciples were with him, and when he came down the multitudes followed, who had not been able to climb it.","14%","30%"],
 ["The disciples following","They come down behind him in a close group. The Sermon ends and the miracles begin in the next verse, so that, as St Jerome puts it, the teaching that had gone before might be confirmed by the works that came after.","36%","10%"]],

'File:Iomenos Gon Ekatonarchou Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The band names the scene: Christ healing the son of the centurion. The Greek word Matthew uses, παῖς, means both boy and servant, and the Athonite painter has read it as son.","6%","60%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","He heals from where he stands, and the painter has put a low wall between them: the hand comes out over it, fingers spread, and goes no further. The centurion had said, speak the word only, and my servant shall be healed — and Christ never goes to the house at all: the whole miracle happens at a distance.","58%","50%"],
 ["The centurion in armour","He is painted in the plate and helmet of a soldier, an officer of the occupying power. St Augustine calls him the first fruits of the Gentiles, and says that beside his faith all the faith of the Jews was unbelief — he had neither heard Christ teach nor seen the leper cleansed.","38%","68%"],
 ["His hands held out","His argument was made out of his own trade: I am a man under authority, having soldiers under me, and I say to this man, Go, and he goeth. He recognised in Christ the one thing a soldier understands, which is a command that is simply obeyed.","55%","74%"],
 ["The boy on the bed","At the edge of the frame he is painted upright behind a great red coverlet, fair-haired, in a green tunic, his face turned up toward Christ — not lying, and with nobody in the picture touching him. His master's words — Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest come under my roof — are said by every Orthodox Christian before communion to this day.","62%","90%"]],

"File:Monreale - Jesus heals Simon's mother in law.jpg":[
 ["The inscription","IHS LIBERAT SOCRUM SIMONIS A MAGNIS FEBRIBUS — Jesus frees Simon's mother-in-law from a great fever. Matthew gives the whole miracle in two verses, and the mosaic gives it a wall.","6%","50%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","He touched her hand, and the fever left her — and the mosaicists have stopped one instant short of it: his hand comes out of the gold sleeve, hers reaches back from the bed, and a band of bare gold lies between the two. St John Chrysostom notes that he does not always heal the same way, sometimes by a word at a distance, as with the centurion's servant just before, and sometimes by reaching out, as here.","60%","48%"],
 ["The woman rising","She is already sitting up as he touches her. The detail Matthew keeps is what she did next: she arose, and ministered unto them — the strength came back whole and at once, and she spent it on other people.","50%","82%"],
 ["The bed","The white coverlet takes up the lower right of the mosaic, so that what she is getting up from stays in the picture. A fever was one of the things medicine could sometimes cure; what could not be done was to make a woman well and strong in the same instant.","73%","70%"],
 ["The house","This is a fisherman's house at Bethsaida, and Christ has walked into it. St John Chrysostom's remark is that he did not scorn to enter the humble hut of a fisherman, teaching us in everything to trample on human pride.","25%","50%"],
 ["The disciples crowded at the edge","Five faces are pressed into the left edge of the mosaic against a painted wall — no doorway — the white-bearded one in front with his hand up in speech. Peter is among them, and he had not asked. St John Chrysostom sees his reverence in that: his own mother-in-law lay sick at home, and he waited until the teaching was finished and the others healed.","48%","12%"]],

'File:Iomenos Daimonizomenous Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The plural titulus","The Greek reads ΙΩΜΕΝΟΣ ΤΟΥΣ ΔΑΙΜΟΝΙΖΟΜΕΝΟΥΣ — healing those possessed with devils, in the plural. Matthew alone says there were two; the Athonite painter has followed Matthew and painted two.","6%","55%"],
 ["The two men","They are painted wasted to the ribs, each in a loincloth — one ochre, one pink — and they are not in the same state: the first is bent double with his arms hanging, the second stands bolt upright with both arms thrown above his head. Matthew says they were exceeding fierce, so that no man might pass by that way — the whole road had been given up to them.","45%","78%"],
 ["The devils coming out","Small black shapes leave the men and scatter into the air. Orthodox painting never gives evil bulk or beauty: it is drawn small, dark and already losing.","38%","67%"],
 ["The tomb","A rock-cut grave stands open at the foot of the fresco. They came out of the tombs, and St John Chrysostom says the detail was kept to correct a superstition — that the spirits crying out of these men were the souls of the dead, which they are not.","84%","72%"],
 ["The herdsmen on the hill","Two small figures in wide straw hats look out from behind the rocks on the hill, the keepers of the swine; the painter has given them no swine and no flight. They fled into the city and told everything; and the whole city came out and besought Christ to depart out of their coasts.","18%","52%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","He speaks one word — Go — and does not come near them. The devils had asked to be sent into the herd rather than into the deep, and the icon shows the command given at a distance.","45%","43%"]],

'File:Christos Iomenos Daimonon Takophon Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The inscription is τὸν δαιμονῶντα κωφόν — the dumb demoniac. St Jerome notes that the Greek word is used indifferently for deaf and for dumb; Matthew's point is that the man could neither hear the preaching nor ask for himself.","6%","60%"],
 ["The hand of Christ at his ear","One hand only, and it is laid on his EAR — the fingers are on it, the first knuckle against his hair, and a white scroll is closed in Christ's other hand. The painter has chosen the ear rather than the mouth, which is the sense of the word over his head: κωφός covers deaf and dumb together. St John Chrysostom says Christ asked this man for no faith before healing him, because the devil had chained his spirit together with his tongue — there was no way for him to answer.","45%","52%"],
 ["The man himself","He stands bowed and clothed, in a green tunic under a red mantle, one hand at his own breast and the other hanging open. Nobody holds him and nobody is bringing him: he is on his feet, alone, in front of Christ. He could not come; when the devil was cast out, the dumb spake, and that is the first thing he ever does in the Gospel.","55%","60%"],
 ["The man arguing at the right","Someone in the crowd is gesturing rather than wondering. This is the miracle after which the Pharisees said, He casteth out devils through the prince of the devils — the accusation Christ answers at length in chapter 12.","36%","82%"],
 ["The disciples behind Christ","They stand close together at his shoulder. This is the last of the ten miracles Matthew collects into chapters 8 and 9, and the sending out of the Twelve follows immediately after it.","30%","10%"]],

'File:Hrist utišava buru na moru, manastir Gračanica.jpg':[
 ["The Slavonic inscription","Three lines in the upper corner say what is happening, in Church Slavonic: Х͞Ъ ВЪ КОРАБИ ПОВЕЛѢВАЮТЬ ВѢТРȢ ДА ОУМЛЪЧИТЬ — Christ in the ship commands the wind to be still. Gračanica was built by King Milutin about 1321, and its painters worked in Serbian for a Serbian church while keeping the Greek forms exactly.","8%","14%"],
 ["Christ standing to rebuke the sea","He stands upright at the left end of the boat under the cruciform halo, ΙΣ ΧΣ lettered over his shoulder, his right hand raised toward the waves and a closed scroll in his left. Then he arose, and rebuked the winds and the sea; and there was a great calm — the same verb the Gospels use for casting out a devil is used here of the storm.","48%","27%"],
 ["Christ asleep in the stern","At the right of the same boat he is painted a second time, lying in the stern with his cheek propped on his hand, while a disciple leans in over him with an outstretched arm to wake him. Byzantine painting shows two moments in one frame; and St John Chrysostom says the sleep was allowed so that there would be time for the disciples' fear to rise, since a storm during his waking would have frightened nobody.","51%","80%"],
 ["The disciples at the ropes","They are painted working and looking up at once. St John Chrysostom notes that they did not wake him gently but violently, crying Lord, save us: we perish.","48%","52%"],
 ["The sail and the mast","The rigging cuts across the whole upper half of the fresco. Everything a crew of fishermen knew how to do is in the picture, and none of it is any use.","24%","30%"],
 ["The waves","Byzantine painters draw water as hard scrolling ridges rather than as motion. What is dangerous in this icon is not the wave but the fear: he said to them, Why are ye fearful, O ye of little faith?","86%","45%"]],

"File:Mosaic of the exorcism of the Gerasene demoniac from the Basilica of Sant'Apollinare Nuovo.jpg":[
 ["Christ without a beard","In the sixth century Christ was still often painted as a young man without a beard, in a philosopher's purple. The bearded face the Church settled on came later; what is already fixed here is the cross set into the halo, which belongs to no one else.","22%","37%"],
 ["The hand of command","He does not touch the man and does not struggle with the spirits. The devils speak first in Matthew — art thou come hither to torment us before the time? — and the whole exchange is settled with one word.","40%","47%"],
 ["The man among the rocks","He sits on the bare stone with his arms out, naked but for a cloth. Matthew names two men here; Mark and Luke tell it of one, and St Augustine's explanation is that the one they name was a man of note, for whom the whole country was in grief.","60%","55%"],
 ["The swine going into the water","The herd runs down the steep place into the sea at the right of the mosaic. The devils asked to be sent into them, and were; and the loss of the herd is what made the city ask Christ to leave.","62%","80%"],
 ["The disciple at his side","One apostle stands close behind him in white. Christ had crossed the lake into the country of the Gadarenes, which is Gentile ground: this is the first miracle worked on that side of the water.","30%","20%"]],

'File:Christos Iomenos Paralyton Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The band reads: Christ healing the paralytic. Matthew places the miracle in Capernaum, which he calls Christ's own city — the town, as St John Chrysostom says, that received him to live there, as Bethlehem received him to be born and Nazareth to be brought up.","6%","45%"],
 ["The word of Christ","He speaks from where he stands, with his hand out. What he says first is not Rise but Son, be of good cheer; thy sins be forgiven thee — and the healing is added afterwards as the proof of it.","57%","36%"],
 ["The man carrying his bed","Arise, take up thy bed, and go unto thine house. The bed that carried him is now carried by him, and the icon puts the whole weight of it across his shoulders where nobody can miss it.","40%","58%"],
 ["What the miracle was for","That ye may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins — the visible thing is done so that the invisible one may be believed. St John Chrysostom's point is that Christ here forces his enemies to admit that he does what only God does.","30%","62%"],
 ["The sick still lying","Others remain on their beds under the portico at the right. The Gospel heals one man in this scene and leaves the room full; Matthew's chapter is not a report of a cure but of an authority.","48%","82%"],
 ["The disciples","They stand behind him in a close group, as they do in every scene of these two chapters. Matthew gathers ten miracles here, and the Twelve are being taught by watching before they are sent out in chapter 10.","35%","12%"]],

'File:Monreale - healing of paralytic.jpg':[
 ["The men on the roof","Two men lean over the parapet with a rope in each hand and lower the bed into the room, and the Latin lettered beside them says so: APERVERVNT TECTVM, they uncovered the roof. Matthew says only that they brought him — the digging open of the roof is St Mark's and the tiling is St Luke's. The mosaicist has followed the fuller account, and the point of it is that they got the man in front of Christ by whatever means there were.","16%","45%"],
 ["Christ enthroned","He is seated and does not rise. Jesus, seeing their faith — the faith of the men who carried him — said unto the sick of the palsy, Son, be of good cheer; thy sins be forgiven thee.","52%","36%"],
 ["The paralytic on his bed","He is painted at the moment of sitting up, half raised on the mattress. He asked for nothing in the Gospel and said nothing; everything is done for him and then to him.","64%","58%"],
 ["The scribes","The group at the right is muttering. Certain of the scribes said within themselves, This man blasphemeth — and Matthew records that Christ answered a thought that had not been spoken aloud.","42%","80%"],
 ["The bed","The frame with its cushion takes the width of the lower field. Whether is easier, to say, Thy sins be forgiven thee; or to say, Arise, and walk? The bed is the answer to the question.","76%","58%"]],

'File:Healing of the Paralytic 04-17.jpg':[
 ["Christ speaking","Dionisy paints him at the left with his hand raised to the man across the fresco. Ferapontov, in 1502, is one of the very few Orthodox programmes that paint the Gospel's teaching scenes as well as its feasts.","27%","30%"],
 ["The man carrying his bed away","He walks off with the whole frame on his back, and the painter has made it heavy. It is the sign Christ chose to give, in front of the men who had just accused him of blasphemy.","33%","53%"],
 ["The same man still lying","At the lower left he is painted a second time, flat on the bed with people around him. The two moments are set in one frame, because an icon holds a whole event still rather than stopping it at an instant.","66%","25%"],
 ["Those who carried him","The group bent over the bed are the friends whose faith Matthew mentions: Jesus, seeing their faith, said unto the sick of the palsy, Son, be of good cheer. It is the only faith the passage records.","58%","20%"],
 ["The men standing apart","At the right stand figures in dark robes who do not move. They are the scribes of Matthew 9:3, and the crowd's answer at the end of the passage is set against them: they marvelled, and glorified God, which had given such power unto men.","45%","82%"]],

'File:090 Mathew the Apostle Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["The plain gold halo","The circle behind his head has no cross in it. Only Christ's halo carries the cross and the letters Ο ΩΝ; a saint's is left plain, because the light in it is given to him and not his own.","15%","50%"],
 ["The pen","He is painted at work, holding the reed. The Church keeps him as an Evangelist as well as an Apostle: this Gospel is his, and the passage the icon belongs to is the one where he writes down his own calling.","50%","45%"],
 ["The book","The book is shut: gold covers tooled with a running border, a red spine with raised bands, the page-block showing, held upright against his knee. By the convention of the Evangelist portrait it is his own Gospel, but nothing is open and nothing is written on it in this panel. St Jerome notes that he alone gives himself both names in it — Matthew and the publican — where the other Evangelists spare him and write Levi; he accuses himself so that no one turning to better things need despair.","65%","72%"],
 ["An old man's face","He is not painted as the tax-gatherer at the toll booth but as the apostle he became, at the end of a long life. St John Chrysostom points at the obedience in the calling: he neither refused, nor asked leave to go home first, but arose and followed him.","24%","58%"]],

'File:Matthew the Evangelist - icon.jpeg':[
 ["The Slavonic inscription","The gold letters read: Holy Evangelist Matthew. Palekh painted for Russian parishes into the nineteenth century in a manner still fully Byzantine in its grammar, however soft the faces became.","9%","25%"],
 ["The quill and the open book","He is caught in the act of writing, the pen lifted from the page. The Gospel is not shown as a finished object to be carried but as something being written by a particular man at a particular desk.","40%","16%"],
 ["The angel behind him","Each Evangelist is given one of the four living creatures of Ezekiel and the Apocalypse, and Matthew is given the man. St Jerome states the reason plainly: the face of a man signifies Matthew, who accordingly opens his Gospel with the human genealogy of Christ.","14%","78%"],
 ["The written sheet in his hand","Lines of Slavonic are legible on the page he holds up. The icon shows a book with words in it, not a symbol of a book: what the Church venerates here is a text.","50%","30%"],
 ["His head turned back","He looks away from the page toward the angel at his shoulder. The Gospels are held to be written by men who were listening — the hand is Matthew's, and the icon says where the words came from.","18%","55%"]],

'File:Daughter of the head of synagogue is resurrected by Christ.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","The Latin runs IHS FILIAM IAYRI PRINCIPIS SYNAGE IN DOMO EIVS SVSCITAT — Jesus raises the daughter of Jairus, ruler of the synagogue, in his house. Matthew never gives the man a name; he is a certain ruler in this Gospel, and IAYRI comes from St Mark and St Luke. St John Chrysostom points out how badly this one miracle would tell against the Pharisees — the man who came begging for it was a ruler of their own synagogue.","6%","45%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","He took her by the hand, and the maid arose. He does not speak from the door; the whole scene turns on the two hands meeting, which under the Law was contact with a corpse and uncleanness.","56%","48%"],
 ["The girl","She is twelve years old, the age at which St John Chrysostom notes the flower of youth begins, and she was her father's only child. In the mosaic she is already sitting bolt upright on the bed with her long sleeve stretched out and her hand closed in his, her face turned up to him.","58%","72%"],
 ["The crowd at the door","The mourners and the minstrels are painted crowded at the right. Matthew says he put the people forth before he went in — the noise of professional grief is turned out of the room before the miracle.","40%","82%"],
 ["The bed","The bed with its heavy coverlet fills the bottom of the mosaic. He had said, the maid is not dead, but sleepeth — and they laughed him to scorn.","76%","55%"]],

"File:Raising of Jairus' daughter 03-19.jpg":[
 ["Christ at the bed","At the left he stands over the girl with his hand out. Dionisy has painted two moments on one wall, and this is the second of them.","33%","38%"],
 ["The girl on the bed","She is already sitting up, in white, reaching out so that her hand and his almost meet across the coverlet — he went in, and took her by the hand, and the maid arose. Matthew compresses the story: the father says at the start that she is already dead, and St Augustine explains that Matthew has given the man's mind rather than his exact words.","50%","28%"],
 ["The parents behind the bed","Two figures only stand behind the bed, a woman in a white headcloth and a bearded man in white. Dionisy has not painted the minstrels and the people making a noise that Matthew mentions: by this moment in the story they have been put forth.","36%","14%"],
 ["Christ again, at the right","The same figure is painted a second time on the other side of the fresco, in the street. This is the earlier moment — Jesus arose, and followed him, and so did his disciples.","28%","70%"],
 ["Jairus at his feet","The small kneeling figures are the ruler of the synagogue, who came and worshipped him. He asked for a hand laid on a dead child, and the Gospel calls that worship.","57%","61%"]],

'File:Tokalı Kilise Raising of daughter of Jairus - 2004 6964.jpg':[
 ["The cross in the halo","Even in a tenth-century cave church in Cappadocia, worn almost to the plaster, the bars of the cross are still visible in the halo. It is how the eye finds Christ in a crowd of haloed figures, and it is reserved to him alone.","11%","39%"],
 ["His hand reaching down","He bends over the bed and puts his hand out above the wrapped body. In every other version of this scene he takes her by the hand; here he cannot, because the painter has wound her up to the chin and left her no hand to take.","37%","40%"],
 ["The girl","She lies wound head to foot in a blue-and-white striped shroud, with nothing of her showing. Matthew's word for what he did to her is the word the Gospels use of the Resurrection — she was not woken, she was raised.","46%","42%"],
 ["The great bed","The painter has given most of the wall to the bed and its patterned cover, so that the reader is looking at a deathbed before he sees anything else in the scene.","70%","45%"],
 ["The apostles behind him","They stand pressed together at his back, watching and doing nothing. Matthew says his disciples followed him into the house — this raising is one of the things they will be sent out to do in his name in the next chapter.","10%","30%"]],

'File:Christos Iomenos Typhlon.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","Ο ΧΣ ΙΩΜΕΝΟΣ ΤΟΝ ΤΥΦΛΟΝ — Christ healing the blind man, in the singular. Matthew's passage has two men crying together; the inscription and the painting have one, and reading the titulus is the only way to know which blind man an Athonite fresco means.","5%","50%"],
 ["The hand at his eyes","Christ reaches to the man's face. In Matthew's account he touches their eyes and says, According to your faith be it unto you — the only condition named in the whole miracle.","42%","45%"],
 ["The staff","The man is still holding the stick he was led by. Orthodox icons keep the instrument of the affliction inside the frame after the cure, so that what has been changed can be seen.","68%","45%"],
 ["The man washing at the basin","At the right the same man bends over a cross-shaped pool and washes his face. This is the pool of Siloam, and it belongs to the man born blind of John 9, whom the Church reads on the Sunday of the Blind Man — the Athonite cycle follows its lectionary rather than Matthew's order.","52%","80%"],
 ["The disciples","They stand behind Christ in a block, as they do through the whole of this fresco cycle. In Matthew the two blind men follow him into the house before he touches them; the healing is not done in the street.","36%","12%"]],

'File:Iomenos xeran echon cheira Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The band reads: Christ healing the man who had the withered hand. St Jerome sets the scene in the argument — the Pharisees had failed to convict the disciples over the ears of corn, so they now looked for a charge against Christ himself.","5%","45%"],
 ["Christ seated in the synagogue","He is painted seated, in their own building, on their own sabbath. St Hilary notices the change of place: what had gone before was said in the open air, and this was done after he had gone inside.","45%","20%"],
 ["The withered hand held out","Then saith he to the man, Stretch forth thine hand. And he stretched it forth; and it was restored whole, like as the other. Nothing is touched and nothing is applied — the man is told to do the one thing he cannot do, and finds that he can.","38%","41%"],
 ["The man standing in the middle","He is set upright in the centre of the fresco with everyone looking at him. The healing is deliberately public: Christ asks them first whether it is lawful to do well on the sabbath days, and answers his own question in front of them.","62%","45%"],
 ["The Pharisees seated","Three grey-bearded men are gathered at the right, and they are not still: one has his hand up with the palm out, one leans forward with his hand thrown toward his neighbour, and the third sits with his hand at his breast and his eyes down. Matthew's next sentence is the point of their stillness: then the Pharisees went out, and held a council against him, how they might destroy him.","46%","72%"]],

'File:Iomenos Daimonizomenon Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The inscription is singular — Christ healing the one possessed with a devil. Matthew says the man was blind and dumb as well as possessed, and that after the healing both spake and saw.","15%","33%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","One gesture across the width of the fresco. St John Chrysostom's remark on this miracle is about what the devil had done: he had blocked both the ways by which a man could come to believe, hearing and sight, and Christ opened them both.","52%","35%"],
 ["The man","He stands stripped and bent at the left, his arms up. Orthodox painting does not make the possessed monstrous; he is a man in a bad state, and he is at the edge of the frame rather than the centre.","65%","18%"],
 ["The devil going out","A small black imp with spread wings and thin crooked limbs hangs over the man's head, and a black streak runs down out of it into his open mouth. It is given no size, no beauty and no dignity, which is itself the teaching: the icon will not let evil look impressive.","42%","24%"],
 ["The apostles","They stand behind Christ, closed in a group. What follows this miracle in Matthew is the charge that he cast out devils by Beelzebub, and the warning about the sin against the Holy Spirit that is answered to it.","28%","80%"]],

'File:Christos Pantokrator Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["Ο ΩΝ in the halo","The letters set into the three visible arms of the cross read Ο ΩΝ — He Who Is, the name spoken from the burning bush. Painted on this face, they say that the man in the dome is the God who gave Moses that name.","17%","50%"],
 ["ΙΣ ΧΣ","The two abbreviations on either side of the head name him: Ἰησοῦς Χριστός. An Orthodox image is not complete until it is inscribed, because the honour paid to it passes to the person named and not to the paint.","28%","30%"],
 ["The hand of blessing","The fingers are shaped to spell the name and to confess the faith — two fingers for the two natures, three touching for the Trinity. This is the hand held out over the words, Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden.","55%","22%"],
 ["The closed Gospel","He holds the book shut and clasped, in gold and jewels. In the dome the Gospel is not being read out but held: the Pantocrator is the Ruler of all, and what he holds is the law by which everything under the dome will be judged.","70%","60%"],
 ["The inscribed band","A ring of Greek runs right round the medallion, and the words legible along the bottom of it are ΤΩΝ ΔΥΝΑΜΕΩΝ — the close of the invocation Κύριε ὁ Θεὸς τῶν δυνάμεων, O Lord God of hosts. The whole church is built under this circle, and everyone in it stands inside the sentence.","88%","45%"]],

'File:The Twelve Apostles, with later gilding, Greek, Late Byzantine, early 1300s, tempera and gold leaf on wood panel - Princeton University Art Museum - DSC06701.jpg':[
 ["Twelve, ranked and standing","There is no scene here and nothing is happening: twelve men are simply set out in rows. The Church calls this the Synaxis, the assembly, and keeps the Synaxis of the Twelve on the 30th of June, the day after Peter and Paul.","25%","50%"],
 ["The gold ground","No landscape, no building, no hour of the day. Gold in an icon is not a background but the absence of one — the men are shown outside the world they were sent into.","12%","50%"],
 ["The front rank","The panel is built as three rows of four, and the lowest row has four in it, not two, painted largest and standing clear of the rest. Matthew names them in order at the head of chapter 10 — the first, Simon, who is called Peter — and gives the whole list before he gives the commission.","55%","40%"],
 ["Scrolls and books in their hands","In the lowest row three of the four are still legible under the darkening: a gold-edged book held at the left, a pale scroll in the middle, another gilt book at the right. Higher up the paint has gone dark past the point where a hand can be made out, so what the other eight carry cannot be seen. Christ's charge in this passage is a speech to make and a work to do: as ye go, preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand; heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils.","62%","60%"]],

'File:Synaxis of the Twelve Apostles Dimitar Molerov Rila Monastery 1835.jpg':[
 ["The rank of haloes","The gold circles overlap in an unbroken line. Painted at Rila in 1835, this is the same Synaxis the Byzantines painted five hundred years earlier — the Twelve shown together as one body rather than as twelve biographies.","12%","40%"],
 ["The rolled scroll","Several of them hold a closed scroll rather than a book. They were sent to speak: Christ's instructions in Matthew 10 are about what to say, where to stay, and what to do when a house will not receive them.","58%","25%"],
 ["The jewelled Gospel","One carries the bound Gospel in gold and stones. Four of the Twelve are also Evangelists in the Church's reckoning, and the book marks them out from those who left nothing written.","65%","62%"],
 ["The cross held in the hand","A small cross carried in the fingers is the Orthodox sign of a martyr. Christ tells them in this same chapter what to expect: behold, I send you forth as sheep in the midst of wolves.","70%","82%"],
 ["Faces that stay the same","Every apostle keeps his own features from icon to icon — this one bald and broad-bearded, that one young and dark. The painter is not inventing men, he is repeating faces the Church has agreed on for centuries.","25%","55%"]],

'File:Prophetes Ionas Ketos Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek inscription","The letters at the right read Η ΕΚ ΤΟΥ ΚΗΤΟΥΣ ΑΝΑΔΟΣΙΣ ΤΟΥ ΠΡΟΦΗΤΟΥ ΙΩΝΑ — the giving up of the prophet Jonah out of the sea-monster. Christ himself made this the only sign that would be given: as Jonas was three days and three nights in the whale's belly, so shall the Son of man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.","14%","70%"],
 ["Jonah coming out of the mouth","He rises out of the fish alive, upright, and unhurt. It is a picture of the third day, painted eight hundred years before the events of Matthew 12 and read by the Church as such ever since.","25%","52%"],
 ["The open scroll","He holds his own prayer out of the deep, open at its first words: ΕΒΟΗΣΑ ΕΝ ΘΛΙΨΕΙ ΜΟΥ ΠΡΟΣ Κ(ΥΡΙΟ)Ν Θ(ΕΟ)Ν ΜΟΥ — I cried in my affliction unto the Lord my God. The whole of it is the sixth ode of every canon sung at Orthodox matins, so that this prayer from inside the fish is said in church more often than almost any other passage of the prophets.","40%","30%"],
 ["The great fish","It runs the whole width of the fresco, and Jonah is small against it. The book of Jonah is read entire at the Vigil of Pascha, in the hours when the Church keeps watch at the tomb.","72%","45%"],
 ["The sea","Water fills the lower half of the wall. The Ninevites repented at the preaching of a man who had just come out of it, and Christ's charge in this passage is that they will rise in judgment against a generation that would not repent at his.","85%","20%"]],

'File:Prophetes Ionas Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The name","Ο ΠΡΟΦΗΤΗΣ ΙΩΝΑΣ — the prophet Jonah, written on either side of his head. He is painted as a prophet standing in the church, not as a character in a story: Athos puts him on the wall among the men who foretold Christ.","6%","50%"],
 ["The scroll","The Greek reads: I cried in my affliction unto the Lord my God, and he heard me. It is the opening of Jonah's prayer from inside the fish, and the Church has built the sixth ode of every canon on it.","62%","80%"],
 ["The hand raised in speech","He is shown speaking rather than acting. His preaching is the point of the passage: the men of Nineveh repented at the preaching of Jonas; and, behold, a greater than Jonas is here.","30%","70%"],
 ["The prophet's dress and bare feet","He wears the plain himation of the prophets, and nothing marks him as a hero. Matthew's chapter is answering men who demanded a sign, and the only one offered is three days out of an old book.","82%","35%"]],

'File:Apotome Agiou Ioannou Prodromou Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The inscription names the feast: the Beheading of the Forerunner. The Church keeps it on the 29th of August as a strict fast — one of the few days of mourning in the calendar that is not for Christ himself.","7%","30%"],
 ["The raised sword","The executioner is caught with the blade still up. Matthew says Herod was sorry, and gave the order anyway — for the oath's sake, and for the sake of those which sat with him at meat.","14%","28%"],
 ["The Forerunner kneeling","The body kneels forward already headless, unheld, its bound hands stretched down to the pavement. The greatest born of women is painted small and folded up at the bottom of the fresco, and the Church's hymns for the day set his death beside his preaching: he was killed for telling a king the truth about his marriage.","58%","48%"],
 ["The charger","The dish is empty. The head is already off and lying on the step above, and the girl is still holding the platter out for it. It was given to the girl at her mother's asking, and she brought it to her mother — the icon shows the errand as an ordinary piece of household business.","70%","62%"],
 ["The crowned girl","She kneels in court dress with a crown on her head, waiting. Matthew's account of the whole affair is short and cold, and the painter has kept the coldness: no one in this picture is angry.","62%","80%"],
 ["The prison","The barred window and the shut door are behind them. John had been in that cell since Matthew 4:12, and it is from there that he sent his disciples to ask whether Christ was he that should come.","25%","72%"]],

'File:103 Beheading of Saint John the Baptist Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","A worn Greek band runs along the top of the darkened panel, naming what is shown. Orthodoxy paints the head of the Forerunner as a subject in its own right, not only as a detail of the banquet scene.","11%","40%"],
 ["The halo","The gold circle is wider than the head it surrounds and takes up most of the board. It is the whole argument of the icon: what lies on this dish is not a trophy but a saint.","25%","45%"],
 ["The closed eyes","The face is painted at rest, without violence and without wounds. The Church sings of him on the 29th of August as the one who preached the Christ in Hades as he had preached him on earth — the death is not shown as the end of his office.","48%","38%"],
 ["The charger","The dish is the one Matthew names: his head was brought in a charger, and given to the damsel. The Church keeps three feasts of the finding of this head, in February and in May.","73%","45%"]],

'File:19 Beheading of Saint John the Baptist Icon in Assumption of Mary Church in Agios Vasileios.jpg':[
 ["The head handed over","The whole icon turns on this exchange in the middle of the panel: the head is passed from one hand to another, over a dish, like an article at a table. Matthew's account is exactly as flat — it was brought in a charger, and given to the damsel.","46%","56%"],
 ["The executioner","He stands in full armour with his sword already down at his side, doing his work without expression. He is a servant of the state carrying out an order; nobody in the icon is enraged, and that is what makes it terrible.","40%","25%"],
 ["The charger","A serving dish out of a banquet is held up to receive it. The head was asked for at a birthday feast, in front of guests, because of a promise made after a dance.","52%","66%"],
 ["The girl crowned","She is painted in court dress with a crown, waiting to carry it to her mother. Matthew keeps the chain of responsibility visible — being before instructed of her mother — and so does the icon.","52%","82%"],
 ["The body at the foot of the panel","The Forerunner's body lies across the bottom, headless. His disciples came, and took up the body, and buried it, and went and told Jesus: that last sentence is what carries the Gospel forward to the next passage.","80%","42%"],
 ["The barred window","The prison stands behind everything, its grating painted in the middle of the wall. Christ withdrew into a desert place when he heard of this, and the feeding of the five thousand follows immediately.","58%","55%"]],

'File:Evlogesis Pente Arton Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The band reads: the blessing of the five loaves. The Church's name for the scene is not the feeding but the blessing — the miracle is located in what Christ does with the bread, not in how many were fed.","5%","50%"],
 ["Christ looking up to heaven","He makes no blessing gesture: the loaves are gathered in both hands with the two fishes laid over them, and his face is turned up to the sky. He took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave. Those four verbs are the verbs of the Liturgy, which is why this icon is never far from the Mystical Supper.","33%","20%"],
 ["The disciple carrying the loaves","The bread is handed to the disciples and they give it to the multitude. Christ had told them, they need not depart; give ye them to eat — the impossible order comes before the miracle that makes it possible.","38%","42%"],
 ["The people sitting on the grass","He commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and Matthew's detail is kept: they are seated in ranks on green ground, not crowding forward.","60%","85%"],
 ["The baskets of fragments","A clump of woven baskets stands in the foreground where nothing hides them, heaped with broken bread; about half a dozen mouths can be counted rather than a laid-out twelve. They took up of the fragments that remained twelve baskets full — more left over than there was to begin with.","80%","33%"],
 ["The child","A small figure reaches up among the seated people. They that had eaten were about five thousand men, beside women and children: the icon paints the ones the count leaves out.","46%","78%"]],

'File:Feeding the multitude, Sant\'Apollinare Nuovo, Ravenna.jpg':[
 ["Christ at the centre","He stands in the middle in purple, beardless, with the cross set into his halo — the sixth-century way of painting him. He is larger than the men beside him, which in Byzantine art is not perspective but rank.","22%","51%"],
 ["The loaves","A disciple at the left holds up a cloth of round golden loaves, each scored with a cross. They are not laid out to be counted — the mosaicist gives a handful — but five loaves and two fishes is all Matthew says they had, and the picture holds nothing else.","36%","27%"],
 ["The fishes","At the right the two fish are held out across the mosaicist's gold. The fish was a sign of Christ himself in the earliest centuries, because the Greek word ἰχθύς spells out Jesus Christ, Son of God, Saviour.","39%","72%"],
 ["Both hands stretched out","He gives to left and right and does not hand anything to the crowd himself. The distribution goes through the Twelve, which is how the Fathers read the Church's own ministry out of this miracle.","33%","40%"],
 ["The empty gold ground","There is no multitude in the frame at all. Ravenna keeps only the essential: the one who blesses, the men he gives to, and what there was to give.","14%","20%"]],

'File:Monreale - Peter Saved from the Waves.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","The letters read IHS SVP MARE ABVLAT 7 PETRV ALLEVAT, the mast cutting through the middle of ABVLAT and the 7 standing for et: Iesus super mare ambulat et Petrum allevat — Jesus walks upon the sea and lifts Peter up. The mosaic names both halves of the miracle, and the second half is the one Matthew alone records.","6%","45%"],
 ["Christ standing on the sea","He stands on the water as on a floor, in the fourth watch of the night. The disciples had cried out for fear, supposing it was a spirit, and his first words were: Be of good cheer; it is I; be not afraid.","45%","25%"],
 ["The hand that catches him","Immediately Jesus stretched forth his hand, and caught him. His fingers close round Peter's wrist, and his other hand keeps hold of a rolled scroll. The whole mosaic is built around that reach: the arm goes down out of the picture's calm into the part of it that is drowning.","63%","35%"],
 ["Peter in the water","He is painted whole, down to his feet, his knees giving way and the waves closing over his legs; his right arm is up and caught, and his left hand is pressed to his breast. He had asked to be called out and had walked; and when he saw the wind boisterous, he was afraid, and beginning to sink, he cried, saying, Lord, save me.","73%","55%"],
 ["The boat","The others are packed into the ship under the mast and the rigging, and the nearest lean over the side with their hands stretched out toward him. And when they were come into the ship, the wind ceased. Then they that were in the ship came and worshipped him, saying, Of a truth thou art the Son of God.","38%","62%"]],

'File:Christos Iomenos Thygatera Xananaias Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The Greek titulus","The band reads: Christ healing the daughter of the Canaanite woman. Matthew calls her a woman of Canaan — the name of the old enemies of Israel — and the whole passage turns on her not being one of the children of the house.","7%","45%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","He never goes to the house and never sees the girl. He answered and said, O woman, great is thy faith: be it unto thee even as thou wilt. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.","48%","42%"],
 ["The woman's hands","She is painted still asking, with both hands out. She had been answered with silence, then with a refusal, then with a hard saying about the children's bread and the dogs — and she took the hard saying and turned it into her argument: Truth, Lord: yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters' table.","38%","63%"],
 ["The daughter at home","She lies on the bed at the right of the fresco, grievously vexed with a devil, in another place altogether. The icon shows what nobody in the story saw: the healing happening at the far end of the distance the woman had crossed.","68%","78%"],
 ["The disciples","They came and besought him, saying, Send her away; for she crieth after us. The men closest to him wanted her gone, and the icon keeps them standing at his shoulder while she pleads.","28%","10%"]],

'File:Monreale - Multiplication of the Loaves and Fishes.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","The Latin counts everything: with five loaves and two fishes he satisfied five thousand men, and of the fragments twelve baskets were filled. Those are the numbers of Matthew 14 — the feeding of the five thousand — and not the seven loaves and four thousand of this passage.","40%","55%"],
 ["Christ giving to the disciples","At the lower left he puts the bread into a white cloth that a disciple holds stretched between both hands. He is not blessing it here; the mosaic paints the handing over. In both feedings Matthew uses the same chain: he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.","68%","15%"],
 ["The disciples carrying it out","They move to the right in a line, into the seated crowd. Christ's words before the second feeding are the reason there is a second one at all: I have compassion on the multitude, because they continue with me now three days, and have nothing to eat.","70%","42%"],
 ["The multitude","Rank upon rank of heads fills the right of the mosaic. Matthew counts four thousand men in this chapter, beside women and children, and notes that they had been three days with him in a desert place.","62%","72%"],
 ["The baskets along the bottom","A row of full baskets stands across the foot of the mosaic. Christ later makes the disciples recall both counts — the five loaves and twelve baskets, the seven and the seven — and rebukes them for still worrying about bread.","90%","45%"]],

'File:042 Transfiguration of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["The mandorla","The almond of light around Christ is the sign of uncreated glory, not of a bright day. St Gregory Palamas defended the Church's reading of this feast against those who called the light of Tabor a created thing: it is God's own energy, and the saints are promised it.","32%","50%"],
 ["Christ blessing","He is transfigured and yet stands still, blessing. His face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light — the change is in what the three were allowed to see, not in what he had become.","15%","45%"],
 ["Moses with the tablets","At the left, holding the law he was given on another mountain. He died and was buried, and God buried him; and here he stands alive on Tabor beside the one who gave him the tablets.","25%","20%"],
 ["Elias","At the right, the prophet who did not die but was taken up in the chariot. The Law and the Prophets stand on either side, and death and life are equally under Christ's feet.","25%","76%"],
 ["The three disciples thrown down","Peter, James and John are painted falling, with their faces turned away or hidden. Orthodox iconography never shows them looking calmly on: they fell on their face, and were sore afraid.","74%","50%"],
 ["The names written beside them","The painter has inscribed the disciples' names in the field. Matthew names all three — Peter, and James, and John his brother — the same three he will take with him into Gethsemane.","58%","82%"]],

"File:Icon of the Transfiguration from Saint Catherine's, Sinai.jpg":[
 ["The white garments","There is no mandorla in this panel at all; the whole weight falls on the clothing. His raiment was white as the light — and St Mark adds, so as no fuller on earth can white them.","35%","50%"],
 ["Elias","The old prophet in the dark mantle at the left. He was taken up alive in a whirlwind, and the Church reads his presence here as the witness that Christ is Lord of the living.","22%","15%"],
 ["Moses","At the right, the lawgiver who died on Nebo. St Matthew reports that the two talked with him; Orthodox hymns for the feast say the Law and the Prophets came to acknowledge him and to yield.","22%","80%"],
 ["The three fallen on the mountain","They are painted sprawling on the rock in three different postures of collapse. The light of Tabor is not looked at in Orthodox teaching, it is endured; the icon draws the upper register perfectly still and the lower one in disorder.","85%","40%"],
 ["The green mountain","The rock is painted as a solid green mass with no path up it. This is a real hill that three men climbed with him, six days after he had told them plainly that he must be killed and be raised again the third day.","62%","50%"]],

'File:Icon of transfiguration (Spaso-Preobrazhensky Monastery, Yaroslavl).jpg':[
 ["The dark mandorla","The glory is painted as a disc of deep blue-green with a paler star-shaped figure inside it, gold rays breaking outward through the dark and gold stars scattered in it. Orthodox painting says by this that God is not made visible by the light: the nearer the eye comes, the less it can hold, which the Fathers call the divine darkness.","14%","50%"],
 ["Elias","The prophet at the left, leaning in toward Christ. He had once stood on Horeb and been told to cover his face; here he stands and talks with him.","14%","25%"],
 ["Moses holding the book","At the right, with the Law in his hands. He had asked on Sinai to see the glory of God and was shown only the back parts; on Tabor he is inside the cloud, facing it.","14%","78%"],
 ["The disciples cast down","Three men fall down the whole width of the lower half, one shielding his eyes, one flat on his face, one scrambling away. Peter had just offered to build three tabernacles, and the voice out of the cloud answered him: This is my beloved Son; hear ye him.","78%","55%"],
 ["The ascent of the mountain","At the left, painted small, Christ leads the three up. Russian icons of this feast often add the journey to the vision: it was a climb, made by four men, six days after the confession at Caesarea Philippi.","52%","25%"],
 ["The descent","At the right they are shown coming down again. As they came down from the mountain, Jesus charged them, saying, Tell the vision to no man, until the Son of man be risen again from the dead.","52%","82%"]],

'File:The Good Shepherd from the Imperial Palace of Constantinople 5th-6th century AD.jpg':[
 ["The beast across his shoulders","A man carries an animal home on his neck, holding its legs. The tesserae are coarse and worn and it is a shape rather than a legible sheep, but the pose is the kriophoros, the ram-bearer, which Greek and Roman art had been making long before the Church borrowed it — and which the early Church used for the shepherd of this parable before there was any icon of the Crucifixion.","17%","42%"],
 ["The shepherd himself","He is dressed as a working countryman, not as a king. The parable in this passage is put as a question to the reader: how think ye? if a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them be gone astray, doth he not leave the ninety and nine, and goeth into the mountains, and seeketh that which is gone astray?","40%","40%"],
 ["The basket at his side","A woven basket with two round pale things in it hangs at his hip: the gear of the trade, painted with him. The Church attaches this image to the passage without its being a picture of these verses: the sheep in the parable is not carried home in Matthew's telling — that detail belongs to St Luke's version of it.","46%","30%"],
 ["The white field of the floor","This is a palace pavement in Constantinople, laid in the fifth or sixth century, and the figure stands alone on plain ground. The last line of the passage is what the Church hears in him: it is not the will of your Father which is in heaven, that one of these little ones should perish.","62%","68%"]],

'File:Ravenna — The Good Shepherd mosaic.jpg':[
 ["The Shepherd enthroned on the rock","He sits among the flock in gold and purple with a halo, which no shepherd ever wore. Ravenna paints the two things together — the countryman of the parable and the Lord of all — and refuses to choose between them.","52%","50%"],
 ["The staff that is a cross","What he holds is not a crook but a tall cross. The image is of the fifth century, when the Church was beginning to put the cross into the hands of the figure she had always painted as a shepherd.","45%","57%"],
 ["The hand laid on one sheep","He touches one animal out of the whole flock. If so be that he find it, verily I say unto you, he rejoiceth more of that sheep, than of the ninety and nine which went not astray.","60%","62%"],
 ["The flock turned toward him","Every one of the six sheep has its head to him and none is grazing. The passage this belongs to is about not despising one of these little ones, for in heaven their angels do always behold the face of my Father.","70%","25%"],
 ["The starry vault above","The lunette sits under a dome of gold stars on deep blue. This is a mausoleum: the Good Shepherd was painted over the dead, because he is the one who goes out after what is lost.","18%","50%"]],

'File:Workers in the vineyard, Minuscule 269 (11th century).jpg':[
 ["The caption ΟΙΚΟΔΕΣΠΟΤΗΣ","The red Greek, cut short after its first letters, stands over the man in grey who is paying, not over the seated man at the door. It calls him the householder — the word Matthew uses in the first line: the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.","10%","42%"],
 ["The man at the door","An older man in ochre sits on a stool before a tall house with a red roof, both hands open toward the payment. No caption names him. Whether he is the householder shown a second time at his own door the miniature does not say.","25%","27%"],
 ["The householder paying out","He puts a gold coin into the cupped hands of the first man in the row, a looped cord hanging from his other hand. In Matthew the lord of the vineyard gives the order to his steward — Call the labourers, and give them their hire, beginning from the last unto the first — but the painter has written householder over the man who pays, as though the lord did it himself. It is his vineyard, his agreement and his money, and the whole parable turns on what he is entitled to do with what is his own.","26%","44%"],
 ["The queue of labourers","Five men stand in a row, some with long-handled tools: the first is already being paid, and the four behind him wait for the same coin. When the first came, they supposed that they should have received more — and the answer given them is a question: is thine eye evil, because I am good?","25%","62%"],
 ["The caption ΟΙ ΓΕΩΡΓΟΙ","The lower band is captioned the husbandmen, and the painter's word is not quite Matthew's here: the men hired in this parable are ἐργάται, labourers, and γεωργοί is the word of his next vineyard parable, where the householder let it out to husbandmen. The band shows the work itself — one man drives a long-handled tool into the ground with his foot, and the others cut at the stocks with short curved blades. A second red caption at the right is abbreviated past reading in this photograph.","63%","38%"],
 ["The vines","The stocks are painted bare and leafless, with no grapes anywhere: this band is the labour, not the vintage. The eleventh hour of this parable is quoted every year at Pascha, in the Catechetical Homily of St John Chrysostom read at the midnight service: let him who has laboured from the first hour receive his due, and let him who came at the eleventh hour not be afraid.","80%","55%"]],

'File:Christ heals two Jericho blind men.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","What is legible of the Latin reads CLAMANTES DOMINE MISERERE NOSTRI — crying, Lord, have mercy on us; the photograph cuts off the opening words. The mosaic quotes what they shouted, and the Church has made it her shortest and most constant prayer.","5%","50%"],
 ["The two blind men","There are two of them, sitting by the way side as Matthew says. They cried the more when the multitude rebuked them, and it was that second cry, not the first, that stopped him.","73%","70%"],
 ["Their faces turned up","They are painted with their heads lifted toward a man they cannot see. What they call him is a title: Thou son of David — which is the confession the crowds will shout at the gate of Jerusalem two verses later.","68%","88%"],
 ["The hand of Christ","It is out, and it stops short: between his fingers and the nearer man's face lie a span of gold and then the flank of the hill. The mosaic holds the moment before the verse — So Jesus had compassion on them, and touched their eyes: and immediately their eyes received sight, and they followed him. Matthew ends the miracle with the following, not with the seeing.","60%","45%"],
 ["The disciples","They are pressed behind him in a block. This is the last thing that happens on the road: the next verse begins the entry into Jerusalem, and everything after it is Holy Week.","45%","12%"]],

'File:005 Entry into Jerusalem Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["Η ΒΑΪΟΦΟΡΟΣ","The Greek title means the Palm-bearing, which is what the Orthodox Church calls Palm Sunday. The feast is named after what the crowd carried, not after what Christ did.","12%","20%"],
 ["Christ on the colt","He rides turned back toward the disciples rather than forward into the acclamation. Matthew says it was done to fulfil the prophet: Tell ye the daughter of Sion, Behold, thy King cometh unto thee, meek, and sitting upon an ass.","48%","45%"],
 ["The disciples following","They come up behind in a close group of haloes. They had brought the ass and the colt, and put their clothes on them, and set him thereon: everything in this procession is borrowed or laid down by somebody else.","45%","20%"],
 ["The boy in the palm tree","A child has climbed the tree to cut branches. He is in this icon because Matthew keeps the children in the story: they cry Hosanna in the temple the next day, and Christ defends them by quoting the psalm — out of the mouth of babes and sucklings thou hast perfected praise.","22%","62%"],
 ["The city coming out","The gate stands open and people press through it. And when he was come into Jerusalem, all the city was moved, saying, Who is this?","38%","84%"],
 ["The garments spread in the way","Figures at the bottom of the icon lay their clothes on the road under the colt's feet. A very great multitude spread their garments in the way; others cut down branches from the trees, and strawed them in the way.","85%","55%"]],

'File:18 Triumphal entry into Jerusalem Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam..jpg':[
 ["The title across the top","Η ΒΑΪΟΦΟΡΟΣ — the Palm-bearing. The Orthodox feast of Palm Sunday takes its name from the branches, and the icon is painted the same way in every century: a king coming into a city on a borrowed animal.","8%","45%"],
 ["Christ blessing the city","He sits sideways on the animal, as every Entry paints him, but this painter turns his head forward rather than back: he is looking at the people coming out of the gate and his hand is raised in blessing toward them. Matthew's own commentary is Zechariah's word: thy King cometh unto thee, meek.","42%","50%"],
 ["The ass","Not a war-horse, and not his own. Matthew alone reports both animals, the ass and the colt with her, and the instruction to say only this if anyone objects: the Lord hath need of them.","60%","55%"],
 ["The disciples","They walk behind on foot. Six days later most of them will have gone; the crowd shouting here and the crowd shouting on Friday are painted by the Church as the same crowd.","45%","15%"],
 ["Jerusalem","The domes and walls fill the whole upper half. He is entering the city that kills the prophets, as he had said in the previous chapter, and he goes in openly.","20%","40%"],
 ["The children on the road","Small figures at the bottom lay out cloths in front of the animal. The Church sings on this day that the children came to meet him with palms, and that they were the first to call him the Son of David.","85%","45%"]],

'File:Entry into Jerusalem (Afon icon).jpg':[
 ["The red inscription","The Greek is written straight onto the gold at the top of the panel. Titling an icon is not a caption but a part of it: what is written is what the veneration is given to.","7%","45%"],
 ["Christ with the scroll","He rides holding a rolled scroll in his left hand and blessing with his right. The scroll is the sign of one who teaches with authority — he goes into the city to teach in the temple, and Matthew fills three chapters with what he says there.","48%","35%"],
 ["The white colt","The colt is painted white and very carefully. Matthew's whole account of the animal is an act of obedience to a prophecy written five hundred years before, and the icon gives it a third of the frame.","72%","40%"],
 ["The boy in the tree","A child climbs to cut branches down. Orthodox Palm Sunday icons keep this figure in the tree without fail, because the children are the ones Christ defends by name when the chief priests are displeased.","22%","45%"],
 ["The crowd at the gate","The people of the city come out in a mass of heads. They cried, saying, Hosanna to the Son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord — the words the Church still sings in every Liturgy at the Sanctus.","48%","82%"],
 ["The garments on the road","Figures kneel at the bottom to spread clothing in the way. What they lay down is what they have; the icon puts the poorest gesture of the feast nearest the reader.","86%","58%"]],

'File:Rossano Gospels - Cleansing of the Temple.jpg':[
 ["The Greek heading","The line across the top of the page names the scene. This is the Rossano Gospels, made in the sixth century — one of the oldest illustrated Gospel books in the world, and one of the very few Byzantine books to picture this episode at all.","6%","45%"],
 ["Christ driving them out","He moves in from the left with his arm up and the apostles behind him. Matthew's sentence is blunt: Jesus went into the temple of God, and cast out all them that sold and bought in the temple.","18%","34%"],
 ["The money-changers' table","A man sits at his table with the coins still on it. And overthrew the tables of the moneychangers — the trade was lawful, licensed and necessary for the temple tax, and it is thrown over anyway.","24%","47%"],
 ["The cage of doves","A wicker cage hangs at the right of the register. And the seats of them that sold doves — the dove was the offering of the poor, which is what makes the traffic in them the sharpest part of the scene.","20%","78%"],
 ["The four prophets below","Four half-figures cut off at a ledge, each with a hand raised and holding nothing at all: their texts are written out on the purple page beneath them, in four columns of Greek. Their names are lettered above — ΔΑΔ, David, twice and both crowned, ΩΣΗΕ, Hosea, and ΗΣΑΙΑΣ, Isaiah. It is the book's method: the event above, the words below, and the joining left to the reader. Matthew does it himself inside the passage, quoting My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves.","48%","45%"],
 ["The purple page","The parchment is dyed imperial purple and written in silver. A book like this was made for a court, and the scene chosen for it is the one where Christ turns over the furniture of a religious market.","88%","50%"]],

'File:Christ banish tradesmen from Temple (Monreale).jpg':[
 ["The inscription","Only the end of the Latin is inside this photograph, at its top left edge: …RIORV EVTIT, abbreviated, the close of nummulariorum evertit — of the money-changers, he overturned. The rest of the line runs off the picture. The mosaic is twelfth-century, worked by Byzantine craftsmen at Monreale in Sicily.","9%","14%"],
 ["Christ overturning the table","His hand comes down on the edge of the money-changers' table top. Behind his head an arm in a gold sleeve is raised with a short gold rod; whether it is his own arm or that of the young man who looks out beside his halo the mosaic does not settle, and no cords can be made out. The scourge is St John's detail — Matthew mentions none. This is the one scene in the Gospels where Christ handles property violently, and Orthodox painting does not soften it.","48%","30%"],
 ["The scattered coins","A little heap of gold pieces lies on the table top beside a gold cup, and at its front edge two bunches of coins, one gold and one of mixed colours, pour over the rim. It is written, My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves.","72%","50%"],
 ["The cage of doves","A cage hangs from a long pole above the traders, and white birds can be made out through its bars. Doves were what the poor brought when they could not afford a lamb, and Matthew names the seats of them that sold doves with the tables of the moneychangers as what he overthrew.","26%","70%"],
 ["The sellers drawing back","Three men recoil at the right: an old man in a white hood with one hand thrown up and a bag clutched in the other, and two young men behind him, the nearer holding his palm out before his breast. Matthew's next sentence is what the cleansing was for: and the blind and the lame came to him in the temple; and he healed them.","44%","78%"],
 ["The oxen","At the bottom corner the beasts go out to the right with no one driving them: a brown ox with the head of another beside it, and in front several grey rams with curling horns. Matthew names no beasts but the doves — the oxen and the sheep driven out of the temple are St John's account — so the mosaic has painted the fuller story over Matthew's.","86%","82%"]],

'File:074 Cursing the fig tree Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["The hand raised toward the tree","He speaks and does not touch it: Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. It is the only destructive miracle in the Gospels, and it is worked on a tree rather than on anybody.","45%","58%"],
 ["The scroll in his hand","He carries the scroll of one who teaches. The point of the sign is given in the next verses as a lesson to the disciples: if ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree.","66%","60%"],
 ["The fig tree","He came to it and found nothing thereon, but leaves only. St Jerome notes that Matthew says plainly he was hungry, and that this is put in to show the reality of his human body.","28%","82%"],
 ["The disciples","And when the disciples saw it, they marvelled, saying, How soon is the fig tree withered away! Their question is about the speed of it, and his answer is about prayer.","46%","20%"],
 ["Jerusalem behind them","The city stands at the corner of the panel. He was on the road back into it from Bethany, where St Jerome observes he had had to lodge because in all that city no one had taken him in.","17%","15%"]],

'File:Fig tree cursed 04-16.jpg':[
 ["Christ speaking to the tree","Dionisy paints him at Ferapontov in 1502 with his hand out and his disciples behind. Ferapontov is one of the few Orthodox programmes that paint the teaching episodes of Holy Week and not only the feasts.","32%","30%"],
 ["The bare tree","A thin pale trunk with dry branching twigs stands in the middle of the wall against the blue, with not one leaf on it. And presently the fig tree withered away — the fresco shows the tree after the word, which is the only state Matthew describes it in.","32%","41%"],
 ["The disciples","They crowd behind him and look at the tree, not at him. Matthew reports their reaction as astonishment at the speed, and Christ turns it at once into a promise about faith and prayer.","42%","22%"],
 ["The second scene at the right","The wall runs straight on into the next episode: a table spread with a white cloth, men seated round it, an old man in a white head-cloth at the end, and a figure in dark red stretched out full length on the floor at their feet. Byzantine cycles are painted as continuous narrative: one register, several episodes, no frames between them.","40%","70%"],
 ["The company at the table","Figures are gathered at the right around a white cloth. Everything in this chapter happens between the Entry and the Passion, in the last days when he taught daily in the temple and lodged outside the walls at night.","55%","65%"]],

'File:Parable of the Wedding Feast 04-14.jpg':[
 ["The king","The crowned figure at the left is the one the parable begins with: the kingdom of heaven is like unto a certain king, which made a marriage for his son. Everything in the story is his — the feast, the invitation, and the anger.","35%","27%"],
 ["The servants carrying in the dishes","Men stride across the fresco with trays held high. Behold, I have prepared my dinner: my oxen and my fatlings are killed, and all things are ready: come unto the marriage. The refusal, when it comes, is not of a request but of a finished feast.","28%","51%"],
 ["The tables","White cloths and vessels are set out across the lower half. Dionisy painted this at Ferapontov in 1502; Orthodox programmes rarely paint the parables at all, and this is one of the few walls in the world where the wedding feast is on it.","65%","35%"],
 ["The guests","The men gathered at the right are the second set of invitations. Go ye therefore into the highways, and as many as ye shall find, bid to the marriage — and the servants gathered together all as many as they found, both bad and good.","40%","68%"],
 ["The man cast out","At the right edge a man in a pale tunic is bent almost double, head down and arms hanging, while a figure behind him puts a hand on him and pushes. He is being put out of the hall, and that is as far as Dionisy goes — nobody here is bound. Friend, how camest thou in hither not having a wedding garment? And he was speechless.","63%","78%"],
 ["The hall","The architecture stands over the whole scene, prepared and open. The parable ends with a man thrown out of it for having no wedding garment, and with the sentence: for many are called, but few are chosen.","16%","30%"]],

'File:Ten virgins 05-15.jpg':[
 ["Christ enthroned","He sits right of centre on a high-backed throne with a footstool, blessing, with ΙΣ ΧΣ written beside his head. The Orthodox Church reads this parable on Great Tuesday and calls the first three nights of Holy Week the Bridegroom services — the Bridegroom of the parable is the one on this throne.","44%","64%"],
 ["The angels beside him","Two winged figures stand at either side of the throne. In Matthew's telling the door is shut by the bridegroom himself; the icon paints the household of heaven standing at it.","42%","77%"],
 ["The virgins","They come from the left in two groups of four or five, divided by a painted pillar, all alike in dress and hair. Five of them were wise, and five were foolish, and nothing in their appearance divides them — the difference is only what they brought.","45%","25%"],
 ["The lamps that are not there","This is where the lamps should be, and none can be made out. The paint has gone in long diagonal losses across the whole left half of the fresco, and no woman in it is now holding anything. The parable turns on the difference: they that were foolish took their lamps, and took no oil with them: but the wise took oil in their vessels with their lamps.","39%","30%"],
 ["The trees behind the throne","Tall plants rise behind him like the trees of paradise. Watch therefore, for ye know neither the day nor the hour wherein the Son of man cometh — the parable is set in Holy Week and points past it.","24%","52%"]],

'File:John Tokhabi, Last Judgment, around 1100. Icon, tempera. St Catherine monastery on Sinai, Egypt.jpg':[
 ["Christ in judgment","The centre of the top register, where he sat enthroned, is worn down to the ground; what survives around it says what was there. A standing figure survives at either side of the loss, which is where the Theotokos and the Forerunner stand in every icon of this scene — the Deesis, the Church's own picture of intercession at the Judgment — and beyond them the apostles sit ranked on their benches with open books on their knees, rank on rank of winged angels behind them. When the Son of man shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory: and before him shall be gathered all nations.","15%","49%"],
 ["The river of fire","A broad band of red runs out from under the place where the throne was and down across the panel. Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, ye cursed, into everlasting fire — the icon makes the fire flow from the throne itself, not from somewhere else.","45%","62%"],
 ["The enemy in the fire","A dark figure sits in a black mass in the middle of the burning, crowded round with small dark heads, and holds a small pale-robed soul on his knees with a gold arc behind them both. Orthodox Judgment icons paint him as a prisoner in his own place, seated but going nowhere.","46%","70%"],
 ["The ranks of the saved","The whole left half is filled with orderly rows — apostles, bishops, martyrs, monks, women. They are painted standing quietly in ranks, while the other side of the panel is all motion.","45%","20%"],
 ["The cells of punishment","At the lower right the panel is divided into squared compartments, some dark blue with rows of pale faces packed inside them, some red with figures in the flame, and a red Greek line lettered over each. This is a monastic icon, painted at Sinai about the year 1100, and it separates the torments by kind and labels them.","72%","82%"],
 ["The written bands","Line after line of small Greek minuscule runs across the gold under the benches, the letterforms clear and the words too fine to read in any reproduction of this panel. What a Last Judgment writes in those bands is the sentence of Matthew 25: I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat — inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me.","34%","48%"]],

'File:Second Coming by G.Klontzas (16th c.).jpg':[
 ["Christ in the mandorla","He sits in judgment at the head of the whole panel. Matthew 24 gives the sign: then shall appear the sign of the Son of man in heaven, and then shall all the tribes of the earth mourn, and they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.","10%","50%"],
 ["The Theotokos and the Forerunner","The two figures turned toward him on either side are his mother and John the Baptist, in the posture the Church calls the Deesis — the entreaty. They are painted at the Judgment because the Church asks them to intercede at it.","12%","40%"],
 ["The prepared throne","Below him stands an empty throne bearing the cross. The Greek name for it is the Hetoimasia, the throne made ready: it waits through the whole of history, and this discourse is about the day it is filled.","27%","50%"],
 ["The apostles enthroned","They sit in two ranks to left and right, twelve of them, each on his own seat. Christ had promised them exactly that: ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.","32%","22%"],
 ["The river of fire","The red stream runs the whole height of the icon, from under the throne to the bottom edge. Orthodox iconography paints judgment as one continuous thing descending, not as a courtroom.","52%","50%"],
 ["The ranks of the saved","Along the left the orders of the saved are drawn up in bands — patriarchs, bishops, monks, martyrs. Watch therefore: for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come.","65%","18%"],
 ["The earth and the sea giving up their dead","Along the bottom the ground opens and the water gives back the bodies in it. Klontzas painted this in Crete at the end of the sixteenth century, and he has left nobody out of the picture.","90%","25%"]],

// ---- The Passion, Matthew 26:6 to 27:66 (added 2026-09-15) ----
'File:Monreale - Anointing at Bethany.jpg':[
 ["Christ at the table","He sits upright at the left on a cushioned seat, haloed, his right hand low at his knee with the fingers reaching down toward the woman on the floor. Matthew sets the meal in the house of Simon the leper at Bethany, two days before the Passover, while the chief priests are already consulting how to take him: the icon shows him at ease at a supper with the plot made.","38%","17%"],
 ["The woman at his feet","She is stretched along the floor at his feet, the only figure in the mosaic not upright, her face turned up to him and her hands at his bare foot. Matthew has her pour the ointment on his head; the mosaicist follows John, who puts her at the feet — though her hair is bound up under a white cloth here, and nothing in the picture is wiped with it. The Church reads every account of her in Holy Week and does not sort them: what it keeps is that she understood what the twelve did not.","78%","27%"],
 ["The alabaster vessel","A small white two-handled vase stands upright on the floor below the couch, where she set it down. An alabaster box of very precious ointment — the disciples price it, and she does not.","86%","12%"],
 ["The murmuring disciples","They are packed at the right with their hands up in argument. To what purpose is this waste? Christ answers that the poor they will always have, and that she has done a good work upon him.","40%","72%"],
 ["The dish on the table","One dish at the centre of the cloth in front of the guests. The supper at Bethany opens the Passion narrative and the next meal Matthew describes is the Mystical Supper; it is at table that he says she did it for my burial.","57%","42%"]],

'File:Judas receiving money, icon of Yaroslavl (19 c, priv.coll).jpg':[
 ["Judas","He stands at the left, bearded and unhaloed, in a dark red mantle over a gold-shot green sleeve, his bare hand laid flat on the edge of the table. A painted column cuts him off from the rest. What will ye give me, and I will deliver him unto you? — Matthew has him open the bargaining himself.","45%","22%"],
 ["The high priest","At the centre behind the table, in the yellow hood and veil of the priesthood, he brings both hands down on the cloth and pushes the money out. Matthew says they covenanted with him: the offer is Judas's, the price is theirs.","37%","50%"],
 ["The thirty pieces of silver","The coins lie spread flat in a single layer on the white cloth between them, a scatter of small discs and not a heap — the money is being counted, not handed over in a bag. Thirty shekels was the sum the Law set for a slave gored by an ox, and Zechariah had named it in scorn: a goodly price that I was prised at of them.","55%","50%"],
 ["The elders","A press of capped and bearded heads fills the right-hand side, with a beardless young man in blue among them and one elder seated in orange in front. The council is painted as a body rather than as men: after naming Caiaphas once, Matthew speaks only of the chief priests and elders.","42%","78%"],
 ["The canopy and the drawn curtain","The scene is set under a draped canopy between two towers, the painter's convention for indoors — the palace of the high priest where they assembled. The curtain is pulled back so that what was done in private is shown.","15%","50%"]],

'File:05 Last Supper Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam.jpg':[
 ["Christ at the head of the table","He sits at the top of the table with the cruciform halo, his right hand up in blessing at his breast and his left reaching down over the cloth. Take, eat; this is my body — the Liturgy repeats Matthew's words at every Eucharist.","28%","48%"],
 ["The inscription","ὁ δεῖπνος ὁ μυστικός — the Mystical Supper — is written on either side of the hanging lamp. The Greek Church does not call this the Last Supper: not the last meal but the first Liturgy.","12%","40%"],
 ["The beloved disciple","John is bowed low at the Lord's side, beardless and haloed, his head almost on the table — one of several beardless apostles here, not the only one. Matthew does not report it; the painter takes it from John's own Gospel, and the two accounts have always been painted as one.","44%","38%"],
 ["The hand across the cloth","At the front of the table one disciple reaches over toward the dish. He that dippeth his hand with me in the dish, the same shall betray me — the painter names no one and paints the reach.","62%","66%"],
 ["The single dish","A deep footed basin stands in the middle of the cloth, with a dark flask and two small cups about it and a knife or two laid across the boards; no loaves are painted. Matthew's account of this meal is the one the Church reads on Holy Thursday, and what is on this table is all that he records being blessed.","52%","52%"]],

'File:Kirillo-Belozersky iconostasis 12 - Last Supper.jpg':[
 ["Christ","He reclines at the head of the table at the upper left, the only haloed figure, with his hand over the disciple at his breast. The panel was painted for the festival tier of a screen, to be read from the nave: the Lord is found first, at the top.","32%","17%"],
 ["The beloved disciple","John rests against the Lord, painted small and close. The detail is from John's own Gospel, set into Matthew's meal; the Church has never painted the Supper without it.","43%","22%"],
 ["The hand stretched to the dish","From the far side of the table an apostle in green stretches his arm out and brings his hand down into a small footed cup standing on the cloth. Matthew's sign is exact — he that dippeth his hand with me in the dish — and the painter gives the man nothing else to mark him: no colour, no place, only the reach into the vessel.","48%","50%"],
 ["The table stood on end","The top of the table is tilted up until the cup and the dishes sit in full view. An icon does not use perspective to lead the eye into the room; it turns the room out toward the reader, so that nothing on the table can be hidden.","57%","50%"],
 ["The apostles in one curve","Twelve apostles run round the table in a single unbroken arc — five of them seated in front with their backs half turned — and every face is in profile toward the centre. Painted for a screen seen from the floor, the composition is reduced to one shape, and the shape is the Church at table.","66%","60%"],
 ["The borrowed room","Two towers at the corners with a wall between them frame the scene, the convention for indoors. Matthew has the disciples sent into the city to a certain man: the room is borrowed, and the icon does not dress it up.","15%","18%"]],

'File:Alektor Petrou Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","Η ΑΡΝΗΣΙΣ ΤΟΥ ΠΕΤΡΟΥ — the denial of Peter — across the top. The fresco tells the story three times in one field, as Matthew tells it: Peter answers three people in three places.","5%","35%"],
 ["Peter at the fire","He sits on the ground across the fire from the others, white-haired, in a yellow robe, one hand up in protest and the other held out to the flames: I know not what thou sayest. Matthew has him sit with the servants; the painter has given him two soldiers in scale armour and helmets and one young man in blue, which is how far in he had got.","52%","27%"],
 ["The damsel at the door","A woman in red stands in the doorway and points. Thou also wast with Jesus of Galilee — the first accuser is a servant girl, and the apostle who drew a sword in the garden is undone by her.","30%","53%"],
 ["Peter at the porch","He is painted a second time, standing outside the door in the same yellow robe with his hand up toward the girl. The fresco gives him one accuser and repeats Peter instead of adding a second maid; Matthew notes that the second denial was made with an oath, and each answer is worse than the last.","35%","42%"],
 ["Peter weeping","At the top right he is bent double over a green column with his face buried in his arms. And he went out, and wept bitterly. The Church reads the bitter weeping as the beginning of his return, and sets it against the remorse of Judas, which had no return in it.","12%","73%"],
 ["The cock on the parapet","The bird stands on the flat roof of a little white building beside him, no bigger than a hand, its head thrown back and its beak open in the act of crowing. Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice — it is painted at the moment its cry is heard, beside the man who remembers.","6%","87%"]],

"File:The Lord's prayer in Gethsemane - Stavronikita monastery, Mt Athos - Theophanes of Crete, 16th c..jpg":[
 ["Christ at prayer on the rock","At the upper left he kneels on the rock, bowed toward the ground, alone. O my Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me. The Church reads the agony as the true will of a true man, freely brought under the Father's.","30%","25%"],
 ["The prayer painted three times","He is painted four times on this wall: bowed on the rock at the left, bowed again at the right, upright at the top centre, and again lower down, turned back with his hand out to the sleepers. Matthew counts three prayers — he went away again the second time, and prayed the third time, saying the same words — and the fresco does not choose one moment; it gives the whole night.","32%","85%"],
 ["Christ returning to the disciples","He comes down the rock, standing, with his hand out toward the sleepers. What, could ye not watch with me one hour? It is said to Peter, who had sworn an hour before that he would die with him.","46%","55%"],
 ["The disciples asleep","A dozen figures lie heaped across the whole foot of the wall — not the three only: the painter has put down the eight told to sit here as well as the three taken farther on, some propped on a hand, some fallen across one another, one stretched out flat. The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak, and the sleep is given as much of the field as the prayer.","76%","55%"],
 ["The rock of the garden","The mountain is cut in jagged ledges, ochre and pale olive where the light falls and near-black along the crags at the top. In Orthodox painting rock is never scenery: it is the hardness of the world, and the light along its edges is the only light in the picture.","50%","15%"]],

'File:Gethsemane Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","Η ΠΡΟΣΕΥΧΗ ΤΟΥ ΧΥ — the Prayer of Christ — runs across the top of the dark sky, and the monogram ΙΣ ΧΣ is written beside each figure of Christ. The one name is written four times because the one Lord is painted four times.","5%","28%"],
 ["Christ kneeling in prayer","At the far left he kneels on the rock with his hands stretched out. Matthew says he fell on his face and prayed; the painter keeps him upright enough for the reader to see the face.","28%","17%"],
 ["The three prayers","He kneels again at the centre and again beside it: three times in one field, as Matthew counts three prayers with the same words. Iconography paints time as space — to read the fresco from left to right is to keep the watch through the night.","28%","57%"],
 ["Christ returning","At the right he stands over the disciples with his hand out to wake them. Rise, let us be going: behold, he is at hand that doth betray me. The prayer ends where the arrest begins.","32%","83%"],
 ["Peter waking","One disciple in yellow half sits up among the sleepers, grey-haired, with both hands open toward the Lord — the one Christ addresses. Matthew has the reproach said to Peter for all three: could ye not watch with me one hour?","50%","72%"],
 ["The sleepers","The rest lie in a heap at the foot of the rock, wrapped and inert. They are given the lower register on purpose: the icon sets the watching above the sleeping.","78%","75%"]],

'File:Cathedral (Monreale) - Right wing transept - Agony in the Garden.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","The Latin band along the top carries the verse: VIGILATE ET ORATE UT NON INTRETIS IN TEMPTATIONEM — watch and pray, that ye enter not into temptation. The mosaicists chose the words spoken to the sleepers rather than the prayer itself.","6%","82%"],
 ["The angel out of the gold","A winged figure flies in level out of the gold from the left, haloed, its right hand stretched out until it almost meets Christ's own; there is no cup in it. Matthew does not mention the angel; Luke does — there appeared an angel unto him from heaven, strengthening him — and the mosaic gives Matthew's night Luke's comfort.","12%","48%"],
 ["Christ at prayer","At the upper right he kneels on the hill with both hands lifted toward the angel. O my Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me — the mosaic paints the petition, and the lifted hands are the nevertheless.","22%","68%"],
 ["Christ returning to the sleepers","He is painted a second time at the right, standing upright above the heap of disciples with a closed scroll in his left hand and his right hand out toward them. The words in the band above are said here: could ye not watch with me one hour?","55%","90%"],
 ["The disciples asleep","They are heaped together across the middle of the panel, more than half of it, wrapped and slumped against one another. Matthew has him find them asleep three times; the mosaicists paint the sleep once and give it the whole hillside.","60%","38%"]],

'File:Prodosia Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","Η ΠΡΟΔΟΣΙΑ — the Betrayal — is written across the top. The Greek word means the handing-over and the treachery at once, which is exactly Matthew's account.","5%","60%"],
 ["Judas's kiss","He comes at the Lord from the left with both arms round him and his face pressed to his. Hail, master; and kissed him. The painter gives Judas no halo: the two heads are side by side, and the difference between them is the light.","40%","50%"],
 ["Christ unmoved","He stands perfectly still at the centre of a scene that is nothing but motion, with the cruciform halo and one hand lowered. Friend, wherefore art thou come? — Matthew's word for him is friend, and the icon keeps the calm of it.","42%","60%"],
 ["Peter with the sword","At the lower right, away from everything else, Peter kneels over the high priest's servant — grey-haired, in ochre, with a short knife at the young man's head while the servant crouches under him in a short red tunic. Matthew does not name either of them; John names both. Put up again thy sword into his place — the icon puts the blow in the corner, where it settles nothing.","76%","85%"],
 ["The staves and torches","Swords, clubs, an axe, a torch and a white gabled lantern with the flame burning inside it are all held up over the heads of the crowd, against a night sky painted black and pricked with stars. A great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people — the painter sets the weapons above the faces, so that the arrest is seen first as a forest of arms.","24%","40%"],
 ["The soldier seizing him","The man who lays hands on him wears no armour at all: a beardless youth in a plain red tunic with a dagger at his belt, reaching in with both hands to take hold of the himation, while the soldier in scale armour and helmet stands behind him. Then came they, and laid hands on Jesus, and took him. Peter's blow and the soldier's grip are painted on either side of the kiss.","45%","72%"]],

"File:Judas's kiss (Monreale).jpg":[
 ["TRADITIO","TRADITIO — the handing over — is written at the top left, and DOMINI, of the Lord, at the other end of the band. The Latin makes a noun of what Judas did; the Greek word, prodosia, means treachery and delivery at once.","8%","10%"],
 ["Christ","He stands at the centre in a cruciform halo with his face set straight forward, not turned to the man at his cheek. Matthew has him answer with a question — Friend, wherefore art thou come? — and the mosaic gives him the still face of one who already knows.","33%","44%"],
 ["Judas's embrace","Judas comes in from the right in a pale mantle, his face pressed to the Lord's cheek and his arm round him, and the two heads are set together in the middle of the crowd. Whomsoever I shall kiss, that same is he: hold him fast. The sign of friendship is made the sign of arrest.","48%","56%"],
 ["Peter and the servant","At the bottom left Peter bends over the high priest's servant, who is down on the ground, and cuts off his ear. All they that take the sword shall perish with the sword — the mosaic puts the blow in the corner, under the arrest, where it changes nothing.","85%","15%"],
 ["The lantern and the spears","A lantern on a pole and a hanging lamp are held up over the crowd, and spears, two long axes and a knobbed club stand up thick against the gold. The arrest was by night, with lanterns and torches and weapons: the mosaicists set the lights and the iron above the heads, so that the crowd is seen as a crowd before it is seen as faces.","10%","84%"],
 ["The man at the right edge","A young man stands a little apart at the edge of the press in a pointed cap and a red-brown mantle over a white tunic, a pole in his raised hand. There is no cuirass on him and no armour anywhere in the panel: the mosaicists paint the arresting party as Matthew describes it, a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.","45%","90%"]],

'File:Kiss of Judas (Church of Saint Nicolas the Orphan).jpg':[
 ["Christ","He stands at the right of the pair, upright and unresisting, his halo painted a deep orange-red with the arms of the cross faint inside it. His own hands are not visible at all: the embrace and the crowd have closed over them. The arrest is already happening round him, and the painter leaves him the only still figure on the wall.","32%","72%"],
 ["Judas","He leans in from the left with his face pressed to the Lord's and his arm across his shoulder. Hail, master. The two heads are set side by side so that the reader can compare them: one has the light, and one does not.","38%","62%"],
 ["The forest of weapons","Swords, axes and spears are raised in a line across the top of the fresco, above every head. A great multitude with swords and staves — the painter gives the crowd its weapons before its faces.","5%","45%"],
 ["The lantern","A lantern burns at the top of the press, a red frame with the light inside it, and the monogram ΙΣ ΧΣ is lettered on either side of its pole, directly over the Lord's head. The arrest was at night, and Orthodox painters keep the lights lit in the Betrayal so that the reader remembers it was done in the dark.","5%","72%"],
 ["The raised sword at the left","A man in a red mantle at the far left has a long white sword up over his head in both senses — raised, and striking nothing. There is no servant on the ground in this fresco and no ear being cut: Matthew's blow is not painted here, only the weapons of the multitude that came for him.","28%","14%"],
 ["The bare heads of the crowd","Not one head in this press wears a helmet and no armour is painted anywhere on the wall: they are bare-headed men with thick curling hair, crowding in under their own raised weapons. Matthew's arresting party comes from the chief priests and the elders of the people, and that is exactly what the painter has given.","28%","30%"]],

'File:Krinomenos Christou Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","Ο ΧΡΙΣΤΟΣ ΚΡΙΝΟΜΕΝΟΣ ΥΠΟ ΑΝΝΑ ΚΑΙ ΚΑΪΑΦΑ — Christ judged by Annas and Caiaphas. Matthew names only Caiaphas; the hearing before Annas is John's, and the painter puts the two trials into one room and names both judges.","5%","55%"],
 ["Caiaphas rending his garments","The high priest on the raised seat grips the front of his robe with both hands. Then the high priest rent his clothes, saying, He hath spoken blasphemy — the icon fixes the instant after Christ's answer, when the verdict is given by a gesture.","55%","17%"],
 ["Annas","A second elder sits beside him in a red hood, one hand raised open toward the prisoner. Matthew brings Christ straight to Caiaphas; the painter, following John, seats the father-in-law at his side, so that the reader sees the whole council that was answering him.","47%","31%"],
 ["Christ standing","He stands before the seated judges, haloed, answering nothing. Jesus held his peace — until the oath, when he says, Thou hast said. The composition is the reverse of an icon of Christ enthroned: the Judge stands, and men sit above him.","42%","70%"],
 ["The book on the lectern","A white sheet covered in lines of imitation script lies on a low yellow table between the judges and the accused, with a black inkwell set beside it — a document, not an open book. They sought false witness against Jesus, to put him to death: the writing-desk is the trial's apparatus, painted plainly.","57%","58%"],
 ["The soldier","A guard stands at his back in a green belted tunic and laced red leggings, his arms folded across his chest and a dark shaft leaning behind him; he wears no cuirass. The painter adds the guard Matthew does not describe, so that the reader sees Christ held while the priests deliberate.","50%","88%"]],

'File:Christ before Caiaphas, GRACANICA 1 090A7764.jpg':[
 ["Caiaphas enthroned","He sits raised on his seat at the left in a round cap, and the whole front of his robe is a mass of torn strips with his own hand still at the tear. The hearing turns on one question — I adjure thee by the living God, that thou tell us whether thou be the Christ — and the painter has gone past the question to the answer to it: Then the high priest rent his clothes.","48%","25%"],
 ["The accuser","A figure in red between the seat and the prisoner points and speaks. Many false witnesses came, yet found they none; at the last came two. The fresco gives the witnesses one pointing arm.","42%","36%"],
 ["Christ","He stands haloed among the guards at the right, silent. Jesus held his peace — Matthew has him say nothing until the oath, and the fresco shows him before that moment, while the case is still being made.","44%","72%"],
 ["The soldier holding him","A helmeted man at his side holds him by the arm. The prisoner between soldiers is how every trial scene of the Passion shows him: he is led, and does not walk.","44%","62%"],
 ["The elders","Behind the guards the elders stand in a group at the right edge. All the council sought false witness — the painter fills the edge with them, so that the prisoner is enclosed on both sides.","45%","86%"],
 ["The cock below the trial","At the bottom edge a brown-and-white cock stands on the crown of the arch, lettering painted on the black ground either side of it. Matthew ends this passage with Peter in the courtyard; at Gračanica the denial is painted directly under the hearing, so that the two go on at once.","92%","17%"]],

'File:Ypestrepseta Triakonta Argyria Ioudas.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","ΥΠΕΣΤΡΕΨΕ ΤΑ ΤΡΙΑΚΟΝΤΑ ΑΡΓΥΡΙΑ — he brought back the thirty pieces of silver. The painter titles the scene with Matthew's own verb.","4%","80%"],
 ["Judas at the priests' table","He stands in red at the table with his hand out over it, emptying it. I have sinned in that I have betrayed the innocent blood. What is it to us? see thou to that — the icon gives the priests the seats and Judas the floor.","50%","73%"],
 ["The silver on the table","The coins lie scattered on the bare yellow table-top where he has cast them down, beside an open scroll of writing, a reed pen and a black inkwell — there is no cloth on it. He cast down the pieces of silver in the temple, and departed — and the priests, who would not put blood money into the treasury, bought the potter's field with it.","58%","86%"],
 ["The priests","Two seated figures at the far end of the table receive him without rising. They took the money back and washed their hands of the man; the painter shows them already turned to the next business.","38%","88%"],
 ["Judas hanged","At the far left, in the same field, he hangs by a cord from a slender arching tree, head thrown back, in the same red mantle he wears at the table, and the fresco letters his name beside him: ΙȢΔΑС. And he went and hanged himself — the two moments are painted as one wall, the confession at one end and the rope at the other.","30%","6%"],
 ["The temple between","A pink enclosure wall and a green building with a red-tiled roof stand between the two scenes, its arched door standing open and a small figure in a red hood leaning over the parapet to watch. The fresco makes the architecture the distance Judas walked.","30%","42%"]],

'File:Judas hangs himself. Gelati fresco.JPG':[
 ["The tree","A single tree with a spreading crown, and nothing else grows in the picture. And he went and hanged himself — Matthew gives the death one clause, and the painter gives it one tree.","12%","60%"],
 ["The rope","A cord runs from the branch to the neck and the head is thrown back against it. The face is painted, which Orthodox painters seldom do for the lost: he is shown as a man, not as a devil.","24%","44%"],
 ["The empty rocks","The landscape is bare rock in steps, with no city and no witness. The scene is set outside everything — outside the temple, outside the company of the apostles, outside the walls.","50%","15%"],
 ["The open hands","His right arm hangs loose along his side with the hand open and empty, the fingers half curled; the left is lost in the folds. The silver he brought back lies on the priests' table in the fresco beside this one; here he holds nothing.","57%","65%"],
 ["Where the picture ends","The wall curves away below the hem of his robe and the feet are not in the photograph at all, so nothing here shows whether they reach the rock. What the fresco does give is the whole length of him hanging, and the Church sets that end against Peter's: both failed the Lord that night and both were sorry, and one went back and one went out.","75%","50%"]],

'File:Aponepsis Pilatou Dionysiou.jpg':[
 ["The inscription","Η ΑΠΟΝΗΨΙΣ ΤΟΥ ΠΙΛΑΤΟΥ — the washing of Pilate's hands — across the top, the painter spelling it with an eta, and ΙΣ ΧΣ lettered in the sky over the halo. The painter names the scene by the gesture Matthew alone records.","4%","55%"],
 ["Pilate on the judgment seat","He sits crowned on a raised seat in the dress of a Byzantine official and holds his hands out over the basin. When Pilate saw that he could prevail nothing, but that rather a tumult was made, he took water — the fresco shows a man performing a rite of innocence in the act of the crime.","40%","30%"],
 ["The water poured","A servant in a soldier's tunic tips the ewer over his fingers into the basin. I am innocent of the blood of this just person: see ye to it. Water washes hands; the icon leaves the reader to judge what else it washed.","48%","47%"],
 ["Christ","He stands at the governor's side with the cruciform halo, hands held before him, silent. The verse here is theirs, not his — His blood be on us, and on our children — and the icon paints him hearing it.","45%","68%"],
 ["The woman behind the seat","A young beardless figure with fair hair bound up stands close behind the throne in a rose mantle, one hand at the seat-back. The head is uncovered, so nothing in the painting settles whether this is a woman or a page of the household. Matthew alone records that his wife sent unto him, saying, Have thou nothing to do with that just man; readers have taken the figure for her ever since.","42%","11%"],
 ["The chief priests","The elders press in from the right, one hand raised at the front of them. They persuaded the multitude that they should ask Barabbas, and destroy Jesus — the crowd is not painted; the men who moved it are.","35%","85%"]],

'File:Flagellation of Christ (icon).jpg':[
 ["Christ at the column","He stands stripped to the waist with his hands bound to the pillar and his head turned aside. And when he had scourged Jesus — the icon gives one verb of Matthew's a whole board.","35%","42%"],
 ["The bound hands","Both arms reach right round the pillar and the wrists are bound on the far side of it, a red cord crossing the wood where they meet. Orthodox painting keeps the body upright and composed even here: the suffering is shown, and the disgrace is not painted into the face.","52%","50%"],
 ["The scourger at the left","Arm back, whip in the air, caught at the top of the stroke. Neither blow has yet landed: the painter shows the sentence, not the wounds.","55%","20%"],
 ["The scourger at the right","The second man mirrors the first from the other side, so that Christ is enclosed between the two strokes. Pilate delivered him to be crucified, and this is the first thing that delivery meant.","55%","80%"],
 ["The column","A dark pillar with a pale carved capital, taller than the men, and the red Slavonic lettering across the top of the board ends …СТОЛПА, at the pillar. The pillar of the scourging was shown to pilgrims in Jerusalem as a relic, and Russian painters give it this plain form.","10%","52%"],
 ["The wall","A flat wall runs behind all three figures with a band of painted triangles across it and a stepped cornice above, and no door anywhere in it. The scene is inside the praetorium; the icon gives the room one wall and nothing else that could draw the eye from the column.","40%","80%"]],

'File:Crucifixion Icon Sinai 12th century.jpg':[
 ["Christ on the cross","The body is laid along the cross in a long curve, the head fallen toward his Mother. Twelfth-century painters used this curve to show a death that is not a defeat: he is not slumped on the wood, he is laid on it.","35%","50%"],
 ["The name above the cross","ΙΣ ΧΣ, the monogram of Jesus Christ, above the cross-bar. Matthew records the charge Pilate set over his head — THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS — and the icon puts the name where the accusation was.","18%","50%"],
 ["The angels above the arms","Two small angels bend down out of the gold on either side of the cross with their hands covered. The hosts of heaven are painted grieving: what is happening below them is beyond their office.","22%","38%"],
 ["The Theotokos","She stands at his right hand in a dark maphorion, head bowed, her right hand held open toward him and her left at her breast — not at her face. Matthew names the women beholding afar off; the Church brings his own Mother to the foot of the cross from John's account, and has painted her there since the earliest icons of the scene.","55%","32%"],
 ["The beloved disciple","John stands on the other side with his hand to his cheek, the posture of mourning. The Mother and the disciple on either side of the cross is the oldest Orthodox composition of the scene, and this small panel keeps it exactly.","55%","68%"],
 ["The saints in the border","Bishops, soldiers, martyrs, monks and women fill the frame in rows. The panel is small enough to have been a private icon, and its owner set the whole Church round the cross: the martyrs in the border died the death painted in the middle.","42%","10%"]],

'File:024 Crucifixion of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg':[
 ["Christ on the cross","He hangs with the arms spread wide along the whole cross-bar and the head sunk toward his right. And Jesus, when he had cried again with a loud voice, yielded up the ghost — the icon paints the moment after.","28%","50%"],
 ["The title","A small dark board is nailed over his head with four letters divided by points on it, worn nearly away — the Greek ΙΝΒΙ, the initials of the title Pilate wrote. Matthew gives it as the accusation set up over his head: THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS.","13%","50%"],
 ["The Theotokos and the women","Three haloed women stand at his right, and the Theotokos is the middle one, in a rose maphorion, held up under the arms by the other two — the swoon that later Greek painting takes from the West. She is not the figure nearest the wood: a woman in a blue maphorion stands between her and the cross. Many women were there beholding afar off — Matthew names Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and the mother of Zebedee's children.","50%","22%"],
 ["John","The beloved disciple stands opposite the Mother with his hand to his face. Matthew does not put him at the cross; the Church paints him there from his own Gospel, and no Orthodox Crucifixion is without him.","50%","68%"],
 ["The centurion","At the right edge a bearded soldier in scale armour and a crested helmet looks up at the cross with a shield on his arm — and he is haloed, like the Mother and the disciple: the Church numbers the centurion among the saints. Now when the centurion, and they that were with him, saw the earthquake, and those things that were done, they feared greatly, saying, Truly this was the Son of God.","52%","86%"],
 ["The skull in the rock","Under the foot of the cross the blue-green rock opens into a black cave with a skull and two long crossed bones lying in it. Golgotha means the place of a skull, and the tradition the Church paints is that Adam was buried there, so that the blood of the second Adam runs down onto the first.","85%","50%"],
 ["The walls of Jerusalem","The city wall runs across the whole ground behind the cross. The painter makes the city the backdrop and keeps the cross outside it: he suffered without the gate.","30%","80%"]],

'File:Double-sided icon - Crucifixion and Hodegetria (9-13th c., Byzantine museum).jpg':[
 ["Christ on the cross","He hangs with the arms out along the bar and the head inclined, the body already sagging in the long Byzantine curve. Matthew's account of the death is the shortest of the four; the icon is as spare as the text.","33%","48%"],
 ["The King of Glory","On either side of the cross-bar the inscription reads Ο ΒΑΣΙΛΕΥΣ ΤΗΣ ΔΟΞΗΣ — the King of Glory. Matthew records the title Pilate wrote as a charge; the Orthodox icon writes what the Church confesses in its place.","30%","20%"],
 ["The archangels","Michael and Gabriel, named, fly in from the upper corners with covered hands. They are painted small and grieving under the arms of the cross.","10%","25%"],
 ["The Theotokos","She stands at his right in a dark maphorion with a gold star on the shoulder, her face turned up to him, one hand closed at her chin and the other held open toward the cross. Matthew's women watch from afar off; the Church brings the Mother close, from John's account.","48%","22%"],
 ["John the Theologian","The beloved disciple stands opposite her with his head bowed and his hand to his face, the mourner's gesture. The two of them at the cross are the oldest Orthodox arrangement of the scene.","50%","75%"],
 ["The stars in the ground","Red stars are scattered over the gold. From the sixth hour there was darkness over all the land unto the ninth hour: the painter puts the stars out at midday.","35%","60%"],
 ["The worn surface","The lower half is rubbed almost bare. The museum records three layers of painting on this face, and what is left of the earliest — ninth-century — is the cross, the body of Christ and the two angels: the composition is what survived every repainting.","80%","40%"]],

'File:Pilate judgement (icon).jpg':[
 ["Christ","He stands at the left in a dark mantle with the gold halo, head a little bowed. The panel has darkened badly and his hands are hard to read — one comes forward at the waist — but no cord or chain is painted on him anywhere. Jesus stood before the governor — Pilate asks his one question, Art thou the King of the Jews? and receives Thou sayest.","48%","17%"],
 ["The guards behind him","Three helmeted heads and the thin shafts of spears rise behind his shoulder. He is brought in under escort from the priests' hearing, and the painter shows the escort as a row of helmets above him.","39%","17%"],
 ["Pilate on the judgment seat","He sits raised on the seat in a crown and a patterned robe, one hand out toward the accused. The governor marvelled greatly that he answered nothing — the icon paints him mid-question.","45%","55%"],
 ["The counsellors","Men crowd the right of the seat and lean in toward it — one in a white headcloth, one in a gold cap, the two in front bare-headed. The chief priests and elders persuaded the multitude — the painter does not show the multitude, only the council at the governor's ear.","42%","78%"],
 ["The vessel for the washing","A figure stands above and behind the seat holding a small vessel: the water Matthew alone records. The painter keeps the washing of the hands in the same room as the hearing.","32%","45%"],
 ["The steps of the tribunal","The seat stands on a raised platform of steps painted in flat colours. Matthew says only that Pilate sat down in the judgment seat; the icon builds the seat as a stage, so that the accused stands below it.","72%","40%"]],

'File:Lamentation icon Nerezi MK.jpg':[
 ["The Mother's face against her Son's","She has taken his head into her lap and pressed her cheek to his. The painter of 1164 did what no one had done before: he let the Mother of God grieve. Every Lamentation painted after Nerezi is working from this.","40%","20%"],
 ["John over the hand","The beloved disciple bends double over the Lord's hand and holds it to his face. He had leaned on that breast at the Supper; the icon of the burial has him take the hand.","47%","46%"],
 ["The body on the cloth","The body lies stretched across the whole picture, the wounds visible, laid on a patterned cloth. Joseph wrapped it in a clean linen cloth — the fresco shows the wrapping about to begin.","60%","48%"],
 ["Joseph of Arimathea at the feet","The bearded man at the right stoops over the feet. Matthew names him: a rich man of Arimathaea, named Joseph, who also himself was Jesus' disciple, and who went to Pilate and begged the body.","58%","88%"],
 ["The jar of myrrh","A vessel stands at the edge of the picture at the bottom left. Matthew's Joseph brings the linen; the myrrh and aloes are Nicodemus's, from John's account, and the painter sets the jar down where the reader will find it last.","85%","5%"],
 ["The hill and the sky","A pale ochre hill against a deep blue sky, with the abbreviated holy names painted straight onto it beside the haloes; the green is the ground along the bottom, not the hill. There is no cross and no tomb in the frame: the painter shows only the body and the people who loved it.","12%","40%"]],

'File:Lamentation by Emmanuel Lambardos (Byzantine museum).jpg':[
 ["The empty cross","The cross stands empty behind the group with the title still nailed to it. The lament is painted at its foot, where the body was brought down.","15%","50%"],
 ["The Theotokos","She holds his head against her face at the left end of the stone, as the Nerezi painter first showed her. The Church sings her lament on the night of Great Friday in her own voice.","57%","22%"],
 ["The body on the stone","He lies on a red slab wrapped from the waist in the shroud. The stone of unction in Jerusalem is venerated as the place where the body was anointed, and Cretan painters give it this red.","68%","50%"],
 ["The woman with her arms thrown up","Behind the Mother a woman in a salmon-orange mantle over dark blue-green throws both arms straight up with the hands open, her hair fallen loose over her shoulder. In the Cretan type the gesture is Mary Magdalene's — Matthew names her first among the women who followed from Galilee and sat over against the sepulchre.","25%","25%"],
 ["John","The beloved disciple bends over the body at its side. Every Orthodox Lamentation places him here, as he was at the cross in his own account.","55%","62%"],
 ["Nicodemus with the ladder","The man at the right stands with the ladder leaning against his shoulder, one hand still holding a rung and his cheek propped on the other. The ladder was for taking the body down; he has come down it and is not done weeping.","30%","80%"],
 ["The nails and the myrrh","At the foot of the stone a round basket holds the pincers and the nails, and a two-handled gold jar stands beside it for the ointment. The painter has signed the board along that same band: ΧΕΙΡ ΕΜΜΑΝȢΗΛ … ΛΑΜΠΑΡΔȢ, the hand of Emmanuel Lambardos. Nothing on the slab is decoration: the tools of the deposition and the burial are laid out as evidence.","85%","58%"]],

'File:09 Epitaphios Icon in Assumption of Mary Church in Agios Vasileios.jpg':[
 ["The Theotokos","At the left end of the body two gold haloes are set almost inside one another, hers and his, which is the Mother holding his head to her face; the paint has been rubbed away and neither face can be read any more. Ο ΕΠΙΤΑΦΙΟΣ ΘΡΗΝΟΣ, the lament at the tomb, is her lament first.","52%","20%"],
 ["Christ laid out","The body lies bare along the slab, head at the left and feet at the right. The surface at the loins is rubbed to the ground, so neither a wrapping nor the wounds can be made out there. Joseph took the body, and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth — in this type the cloth is under him and not yet round him.","65%","55%"],
 ["The empty cross with its title","The cross stands behind the group with the title still on it. He has been taken down: the lament is of the body on the ground, not of the body on the wood.","15%","45%"],
 ["The disciple bending to the body","A haloed figure bends down over the Lord's side. The beloved disciple is at the burial in every Orthodox Epitaphios, from his own account of the cross.","45%","44%"],
 ["Joseph of Arimathea","The white-bearded haloed man at the right bends over the legs and holds them with both hands, one at each ankle, with the bare feet beyond his arm. Matthew says he was rich, and that the tomb was his own new tomb, hewn out in the rock.","45%","72%"],
 ["Nicodemus with the ladder","The man at the far right holds a long rail with short rungs on it up behind his shoulder — the ladder from the cross; his face has darkened past reading. Matthew does not name him; John does, and the Church has always painted the two men together at the burial.","30%","84%"],
 ["The women behind","The women stand in a rank behind the Mother, one with her hand at her face. Matthew names Mary Magdalene and the other Mary sitting over against the sepulchre; the icon gathers all the women of Galilee at the body.","35%","20%"]],

"File:Sealing of Christ's Tomb (Russia, 19th c.).jpg":[
 ["The stone across the door","A great stone, painted as a squared blue slab ruled into panels, is set across the mouth of the cave and fills it. Joseph rolled a great stone to the door of the sepulchre, and departed — the icon shows it in place, and the seal going onto it.","33%","35%"],
 ["The seal being set","The man in the domed orange cap sets his right hand flat on the slab with a small dark seal in it and holds his left hand up as he does it — one hand to the stone, not two. So they went, and made the sepulchre sure, sealing the stone — the precaution was the priests', and the painter gives the act to a priest.","44%","42%"],
 ["The guard at the left","A grey-bearded man in ribbed scale armour and a red mantle sits at the left against the rock with a spear in his hand and a red shield lying at his feet. Ye have a watch: go your way, make it as sure as ye can — Pilate's words, and the watch is the only thing in the scene he supplied.","55%","25%"],
 ["The soldier with the spear","A bearded soldier in a gold helmet sits at the right holding his spear upright past his shoulder, a red shield on the ground in front of him, looking at the seal. The Mileševa fresco beside this icon shows what became of the watch.","60%","75%"],
 ["The second elder","Another elder in blue stands between the guards, watching the seal set. The chief priests and Pharisees came together unto Pilate — Matthew has this done by a delegation, and the painter gives it two men.","50%","62%"],
 ["The women watching","Five haloed figures stand on the hill above the tomb with a line of Slavonic lettering over their heads — a grey-bearded man at the left and four women beside him, so they are not all women. Matthew's last words before this passage are that Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting over against the sepulchre: the painter has them witness the seal they will find broken.","30%","72%"],
 ["The title","The Slavonic inscription along the lower border reads ГРОБЪ ГОСПОДЕНЬ, the Lord's Tomb. The icon is painted from these five verses and from nothing else.","90%","50%"]],

'File:Sleeping soldiers by Christ\'s tomb, Mileševa.jpg':[
 ["The helmets","Six helmeted heads lean together along the wall, five of them with the face still painted and the eyes shut, each in a brimmed helmet over a mail coif. The watch was set to make the sepulchre sure; the painter gives it the sleep of men off duty.","25%","15%"],
 ["The scale armour","The mail is painted scale by scale over the whole group. The precaution is shown as thorough as it was — armour, shields, spears — and none of it awake.","45%","45%"],
 ["The shields","Great oval shields, painted with radiating stripes rather than bosses, are propped among the sleepers — one pale, one dark, one red-brown. Matthew reports the guards' story afterwards — his disciples came by night, and stole him away while we slept — and the fresco paints the while we slept.","60%","70%"],
 ["The spear in the slack hand","A sleeper's arm in a gold sleeve has fallen open and a thin spear shaft runs down across the open palm, held by nothing. The soldiers became as dead men, Matthew says of the moment of the Resurrection; the painter shows them so before it.","42%","61%"],
 ["The edge of the tomb above","Along the top edge the scene above begins — pink-red folds and a corner of white, no more than that in this photograph. At Mileševa what is painted over these sleepers is the myrrhbearers at the tomb with the angel in white on the stone: the watch posted to keep the body in is asleep directly under the empty tomb.","5%","50%"]],
};
